THE ORDER. Will (2026-11-26): "Test enough to understand bubble mechanics, then publish." Instruments: the engine source (hub clone polyworld-v3, games/cac/src - baseset.nim, core/core.nim, core/sim.nim); the 600 local f41 traces (gift2 + control arms) mined by top2/bubble_mine.py plus two follow-up passes; the hosted field corpus (10,242 episodes, ~/field-traces on the hub) mined hub-side. No new probe matches were needed; every question isolated in traces or source. Full record: top2/bubble-mechanics.md.

THE CORRECTION. On 2026-11-26 Emise told Will in chat that a Bubble annuls an attack - "the hit never lands." That was wrong, and this scroll replaces it. Across the 600 local games, 1,596 hero attacks into Bubble-holding boards dealt FULL damage in 1,596 of 1,596 cases; the event order is DamageEvent, TriggerEvent, BounceEvent, DeathEvent (the Bubble's own destruction). Bubble prevents, reduces, and annuls nothing. The set contains no healing, no damage reduction, and no annulment of any kind; hero damage is permanent (0 life-increases across all 600 games) and, against declared attacks, undefendable. What Bubble buys is tempo and board presence: the attacker's body returns to its owner's hand after dealing full damage. The card: Bubble, 0-cost Mage Trinket, never dealt, summoned x2 by Bubble Shield; its whole text is on(attacked(You), bounce(getAttacker()), destroy(self())).

THE NINE ANSWERS. (1) PINGS NEVER TRIGGER IT: queueAttackTriggers is called only at the ends of attackHero and attackMinion; Sharpshooter's ping is a damage(1) rule effect and Post copies re-fire the same effect - 639 non-attack hero-damage actions against Bubble holders locally (Sharpshooter plays 202, Post-copy pings 169, Bolt 178, Overcharge 75, Explode 15), Bubble triggered in 0. The same Sharpshooter's declared attacks bounced 360 times. "Attacked" is a term of art: declared attacks only. (2) MINION TARGETS: firesOnAttack requires a HeroChoice victim; 260 creature attacks against Bubble holders, 0 triggers. (3) MULTI-BUBBLE: one trigger queues per Bubble; they resolve in board order; the first bounces and is destroyed; the second finds getAttacker() Canceled (the attacker has left the board), which per AttackerTarget's contract cancels the WHOLE trigger - runRules rolls its effects back, destroy(self()) never runs, the second Bubble SURVIVES unspent. Locally 372 two-Bubble and 93 three-plus resolutions, never 2 consumptions; the consumed Bubble is the earliest board slot in 465 of 465. Field replication: 21,077 and 19,331, every resolved case exactly one consumption. (4) LETHAL, SPLIT BY GAME END: attackHero settles deaths (checkWinCondition) BEFORE queueing attack triggers, and a game-over clears the queue - a game-ending lethal never triggers the Bubble (local 46 of 46; field 894 of 894). But a lethal that eliminates one of several players leaves the dead player's board in play, and their Bubble FIRES AFTER THEIR DEFEAT and bounces the killer (local 31 of 31; field 1,247 of 1,247). Replay order: Attack, Damage, Defeat, Bubble Trigger, Bounce, Bubble Death. Local accounting closes exactly: 1,596 attacks into Bubble holders = 1,550 triggered + 46 game-ending lethals. (5) BURNOUT: an eachEndOfTurn effect on its owner, not an attack; 1,133 Burnout-tick actions locally against Bubble holders, 0 triggers, 0 Bubble deaths - the clock is blind to Bubble because there is no attack to answer. (6) BOUNCED STATE: in-play state (damage in currentToughness, buffs in bonusPower, gained/lost rules) lives in MinionState; the bounce moves only the printed Card to the home player's hand - damage resets, buffs shed, gained rules shed, lost rules restored; a bounced Post copy returns as the printed card. Replay is an ordinary play at normal playCost; the re-entered minion cannot attack that turn (canAttack = Ready only) unless it has Ready or its owner's live Post re-grants it. Clean same-turn replays locally: 22 of 22 entered at printed stats, 22 of 22 paid printed cost. (7) ATTACKER KINDS: any minion that declares an attack can be bounced, ranged included; summoned tokens are ordinary - hosted Bubble bounces include Primordial x1,409 and Ooze x16,127, and a bounced token becomes a real playable printed card in hand. Even a 0-power attack consumes a Bubble (the Ooze cases): the trigger answers the declaration, not the damage. The only unbounceable attacker is one already off the board when the trigger resolves - then the trigger cancels and the Bubble is NOT consumed. A stolen attacker bounces to its original owner's hand. Local bounced census (1,550): Footsoldier 524, Sniper 362, Sharpshooter 360, Commander 105, Tactician 75, Bear 61, Ambusher 52, Assassin 11. (8) ACCOUNTING: at most one Bubble per attack, earliest slot first, spent one per attack across a turn until none remain. Field totals: 69,490 hero attacks into Bubble holders, 68,596 single consumptions, 894 game-ending lethals, 0 exceptions - no attack taxed twice, none escaping tax while a Bubble stood and the game lived. (9) SOURCE VS BEHAVIOR: no engine contradiction anywhere; core.nim documents HeroAttacked as "after the damage," which is the behavior. The traps are in the card's plain text: "destroy this" reads like a per-Bubble cost (with two up, the second silently cancels and survives), and "when your hero is attacked" reads as "when your hero takes damage" (pings, burn, and the clock all damage without attacking). The annulment error was the second trap, sprung on our own prose.

WHAT IT CHANGES. Bubble's price is tempo - one attacker's body and its replay cost, for 0-2 energy - never damage defense; any record pricing it as protection overstates Mage survival by exactly the damage it "stops," which is zero. Attacking into Bubble is cheaper than priced: the hit always lands in full, lethals included, and a 0-power body can strip a Bubble ahead of a real attacker. Defending with Bubble is narrower than priced: declared attacks only, one per attack, blind to burn and to the clock. Flagged for correction, not rewritten (per the order): weakness-register.md line 25 ("Bubble-protected" - read Bubble-taxed); archer-audit.md line 76 (Bolt ignores Bubble because Bolt is not an attack, not because a blocker fails); design-choices-v2.md D17 (Bubble on sight stands; its rationale re-prices from prevention to tempo).Round 41 - Generation 1: The Learning Champion's First Generation, Closed (EE 916-1000; Stage 5 RECORD + CLOSE EE 997-1000, 2026-11-26)

THE ROUND IN ONE LINE. Generation 1 ran the redesign ledger's 14-variant roster on the v36-phextcore learning core, culled under Will's EE 945 smaller/faster/more-informing shape to six survivors plus CONTROL and the emise-baseline arm, and separated ONE mechanism that moves outcomes: G1-09 gift2's Gift/Clock Export. Its leader shipped as v38-gift2 on Will's word at EE 970, ahead of this formal close (the v30 precedent). This scroll is the close: the verdict under the pre-stated rules, stated whole, including the legs that miss.

DESIGN (Stage 1, EE 916). Base: v36-phextcore = Generation 0 (v33-combined + the libphext-bas phext core; Q16.16 EWMA thetas, gamma 0.875, clamps [0.5, 2.0]). Roster G1-01..G1-14 per design-choices-v2.md Part 2 (inscribed at 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.52): CONTROL, fastdrift, slowdrift, wideenv, narrowenv, earlyrelease, holdfast, tickparam, gift2, gift4, hailpair, scorefocus, compfield, plankeep. Decision rules, per-variant mechanism metrics and falsifiers, the stateful Gate 1 pre-statement, and the advance rule were written in analyze41.py's header BEFORE any tally existed. Guidance adaptations during the round: EE 928 (emise-baseline seated as the standing baseline arm from the deepening passes; the full-manual audit's 9 contradictions bind, C5 zero-failure existential), EE 931 (study / iterate / optimize), EE 945 (smaller, faster, more informing: at most 6 variants per round, 500 matches per combo as the depth unit, paired-seed deltas and per-pass mechanism metrics, cull after the wide sweep), EE 947 (never grind when you can melt sand).

