# Class Audit — ARCHER (Cards and Casters)

**Status: ready-for-inscription** (continuous-training loop: please inscribe at the next free coordinate; this file is the audit of record until then).
Date: 2026-11-26, ~EE 894. Sources: `baseset.nim` (top2/src copy, read in full), `sim.nim`, `battlesetup.nim`, the 507 fully-traced hosted episodes (`traces-all/`), all 630 episode rows (`rows/` + both index files), gauntlet records (Rounds 28–39), weakness register. No candidate built or screened in this pass, per the audit's brief.

---

## 0. CORRECTION TO THE PROGRAMME FRAME — read this first

The framing this audit was handed — "507-episode corpus, 2,264 seat-slots, Warrior 1,221 / Mage 1,043 / **Archer 0**" — is **wrong**, and the error is exact: a fresh count of the setup lines in `traces-all/` gives **2,535 seats: Warrior 1,221, Mage 1,043, Archer 271**. The old figure (2,264) is precisely 2,535 − 271: it silently dropped every Archer seat. All 271 traced Archer seats belong to one policy, **daf-awm-v1:v2 (docxology)**.

The real zero is not presence. It is this:

- daf-awm-v1 plays **350 games** in the 630-episode corpus and wins **0** (every other policy wins ≥7; next-lowest share is the orch filler at 0.109).
- In **271 of 271 traced daf games**, its setup deck is **empty** (`decks[pos] == []`), and its seat reads **`dead: true` in the first recorded game state** — deck 0, hand empty, at tick 1.
- Per the engine's own contract (reference-bot header in every .bas file: "An illegal deck is out before the first turn"; `sim.nim` Seat: deck is "Empty when they brought no legal deck"), daf's Archer seat is eliminated before the first turn for bringing no legal deck.

**The Archer hero has never played a card in the hosted corpus.** The remaining 79 daf episodes are untraced; the deck failure there is *inferred* (identical policy name+version, which is a fixed deck by construction) — labeled unverified, but the traced evidence is 271/271.

Downstream consequence: every prior programme statement of the form "no Archer-class player exists in the corpus" should be read as "no Archer-class player has ever *played* in the corpus." The class's competitive record is not 0-and-lost. It is *unplayed*.

---

## 1. The Archer hero

There is less hero to audit than the word suggests — and that is itself a finding:

- **All heroes are mechanically identical**: 20 starting life (`StartingLife = 20`), 5-card starting hand, no hero power, no class ability, heroes do not attack. Class identity is *entirely* an economy:
  - A deck is exactly 40 cards, 1–4 copies per card, except the three class cards ("Archer" / "Warrior" / "Mage" trinkets, 0-cost, `levelUp(1, <class>)`), which take any number of copies.
  - **Off-class cards cost +3 energy**, reduced by 1 for each class card of that card's class you have in play (`extraCost`, sim.nim). On-class cards cost printed price.
- So "playing Archer" means: the 13 Archer cards below at printed cost, everything else at +3 (mitigable by spending deck slots on that class's trinkets — the tax macro pays 12 slots to avoid).

## 2. The Archer card pool (as printed, baseset.nim)

| Card | Cost | Kind | P/T | Effect (printed rules) | In precon |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Sniper | 2 | Minion | 2/1 | Ranged (takes no combat damage from non-ranged minions) | ×4 |
| Sharpshooter | 3 | Minion | 3/1 | Ranged; on play, deal 1 to any minion or hero | ×4 |
| Ambusher | 3 | Minion | 2/2 | Hidden(1) | ×4 |
| Assassin | 6 | Minion | 5/1 | Hidden(1); on play, deal 2 to a minion | ×3 |
| Bolt | 1 | Spell | — | Deal 2 to a **hero** (heroes only) | ×4 |
| Reload | 2 | Spell | — | Repeat a minion's or trinket's printed rules | ×3 |
| Overcharge | 3 | Spell | — | Choose: deal 1 to a minion **and** 1 to its owner; or deal 3 to a minion, 3 to its owner, and 3 to **you** | ×4 |
| Explode | 3 | Spell | — | Destroy your own minion; deal its power to any minion or hero | ×2 |
| Hail of Arrows | 3 | Spell | — | Deal 1 to **every opposing minion** | ×4 |
| Wildfire | 6 | Trinket | — | Each of your turns: deal 1 to **every minion and every player** (symmetric, yours included) | ×4 |
| Post | 1 | Trinket | — | Lasts 2 turns. When you play an Archer minion costing ≤3: ready it and summon a copy of it | ×4 |
| Gift | 3 | Spell | — | Give control of one of your minions/trinkets to a target hero | ×0 |
| "Archer" | 0 | Trinket (class card) | — | Level up in Archer (discount engine for off-class heroes) | ×0 |

Pool facts that matter:

- **Zero card draw.** Archer is the only class with no draw, no tutor, no recurring card advantage. (Mage: Plan/Study/Overwork. Warrior: also no draw — but see Banner below.)
- **Every minion is toughness 1 except Ambusher (2).** Sniper 2/1, Sharpshooter 3/1, Assassin 5/1.
- **The class owns the meta's two defining engines as natives**: Post (the flood copy engine) and Hail of Arrows (the sweeper) are Archer cards. Everyone else rents them at +3 or pays the trinket tax.
- **Bolt is the base set's only direct hero burn** — 2 damage to a hero for 1 energy, a spell (printed-rules reading: Bubble's `on(attacked(You))` trigger answers attacks, not spell damage — unverified in play).
- **The precon is a suspect build, not a class measurement**: it runs Wildfire ×4 (a symmetric 1-per-turn to *all* minions and *all* heroes) alongside a board printed at toughness 1 — it burns its own board and its own hero every turn; Overcharge ×4 whose strong mode deals 3 to itself; Explode ×2 which eats its own 1-toughness bodies; and it omits Gift entirely (the only Archer card besides the class card left out). Gift's printed text (donate a permanent to another hero) reads as Wildfire's intended partner — donate the Wildfire, keep the burn, lose the self-harm — a printed-rules reading, unverified in play.

## 3. Why zero wins? The four explanations, tested

**(a) "The class is simply weak."** — *Supported only for the stock form, and confounded.* The stock Archer reference (generic reference logic + precon) is Table G's weakest seat: ~2.7% pooled (Rounds 28–36 reproductions), vs the stock Warrior reference's ~7.8% in the same table. But that measurement bundles three things: a self-harming precon (above), generic logic shared by all three reference bots, and the class. No built Archer line has ever been measured anywhere — hosted or local. "The hero is dominated" is **not established**; "the precon + generic logic is the weakest stock seat" is.

**(b) "Its cards are worth more as splashes than as a hero."** — *Strongly supported as far as it goes.* The #2 player's dominant combo (C2, 55.1% of macromackie's traced games, Mage-draw win share 0.403) is an Archer engine rented by a Mage hero: 12 deck slots spent on "Archer" trinkets purely to un-tax Post/Sniper/Sharpshooter. The #1 player's only deviation from the stock Warrior deck imports Archer's Hail of Arrows at double cost (6 energy vs 3 native). The class's cards are not merely playable at the top of the meta; two of the three champion-grade engines in the corpus *are* Archer cards. What (b) cannot show is that the hero is worse than the rental — nobody has run the native version.

**(c) "Its geometry is countered by the current meta."** — *Supported for the precon's board shape; not for the class's full geometry.* The meta's cheapest, densest damage number is 1 (Hail in every Warrior deck; Sharpshooter pings; Wildfire). A board printed at toughness 1 lives permanently inside the band that damage prices cheapest — the weakness register's W2 finding (Hail prices 1-tough kills only) generalizes: the precon's board is the band. But an Archer line is not obliged to field that board: Bolt burn doesn't need a board, ranged bodies trade into creatures *without taking combat damage back* (sim.nim:262), hidden bodies can't be priced while hidden, and Hail — the card that supposedly counters the class — costs the Archer hero 3, hits only *opposing* minions, and is therefore the one sweeper its own 1-toughness board is immune to.

**(d) "Nobody has built a real Archer line."** — *Strongly supported; this is the headline.* The only hosted entrant never fielded a legal deck (Section 0). Our own contact is the Round-1-era v1 Archer starter (pre-gauntlet, pre-replica, never revisited after the v3c Warrior pivot) and the stock reference seat. The exact pattern Round 37 exposed for Mage — "the stock deck may just be unbuilt" — holds here in an extreme form: Mage at least had functioning entrants (A-aron's Mage at 0.203, salvo's at 0.170) before our build. Archer has had *zero functioning entrants, ever*.

## 4. The Archer geometry, priced against the measured meta

What an Archer hero does that neither other hero does:

1. **The flood engine at printed cost with no tax.** Macro's C2 spends 12 of 40 slots buying down the surcharge on Post/Sniper/Sharpshooter. An Archer hero runs the identical engine at printed cost and keeps all 12 slots. Post copies at ≤3 cost are the highest body-conversion mechanism measured in the corpus (replica-Mage: 6.67 copies/game, 23.3 bodies/game) — native Archer gets the engine *and* a real deck around it.
2. **The cheapest Hail in the game (3 vs 6).** Every Warrior-carried Hail in the meta is a 6-energy card, which is why its holders fire it only at huge boards (rado's fires: enemy 1-tough counts p25 9 / median 11). At 3 energy the correct firing window is a different, earlier decision — a priced advantage no current seat holds.
3. **Ranged trades.** Sniper/Sharpshooter attack non-ranged creatures and take nothing back. Against Warrior boards (Bear 3/2, Footsoldier 1/2, Tactician 1/2) a 2-power ranged body removes toughness-2 creatures over successive turns without dying to retaliation — a grinding geometry neither Warrior (trades bodies) nor Mage (bounces/removes at card cost) replicates.
4. **Unconditional face burn.** Bolt ×4 = 8 hero damage for 4 total energy, indifferent to board state, Bubble, and blockers that don't exist in this game. In a meta where the weakness audit's W1 found champions dying with the lowest player spared, a line that can close from hand changes endgame arithmetic. (Magnitude check: 8 damage is 40% of a hero — real, not decisive alone.)

> **CORRECTION — 2026-11-26 (EE ~994).** Bolt's indifference to
> Bubble is not a blocker failing: Bolt is a damage *effect*, not
> an attack, and Bubble's attacked-trigger queues only from
> `attackHero`/`attackMinion` declarations — non-attack hero
> damage never triggers it (639 non-attack hero-damage actions
> against Bubble holders traced, 0 triggers). Bolt ignores Bubble
> for the same reason Burnout ticks do: it never "attacks."
> Full record: bubble-mechanics.md Q1 (softmax.phext
> 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.46).
5. **Ping density.** Sharpshooter's ETB ping under a live Post doubles (copy ETBs too — the replica's 1,052/0 hero-ping discipline is this mechanic). Archer heroes get this at 3, not 3-plus-tax.

Against it, honestly: **no native draw** is the class's structural hole. Macro's flood works because Plan/Study/Overwork refills the hand (15+ draws/game); an Archer hero playing Post copies from a natural-draw hand floods once and stalls — the W3 profile (macro's losses: half-built floods, 30.8% of loss turns ending with ≥3 energy unspent is the *opposite* failure; Archer's predicted failure is an empty hand with energy unspent). And the 1-toughness board means rado's Hail and any opposing Sharpshooter density tax the flood at the cheapest rate in the game — mitigated by Hail-immunity only for its *own* copy of the card.

## 5. Verdict: **UNBUILT CLASS**

- Not DEAD: a dead-class verdict requires a functioning line measured and dominated. None exists. The only measurements are a self-harming precon under generic logic (2.7% stock-ref) and a deckless seat dead at tick 1 (0/350). Meanwhile the class's own cards power the #2 player's engine and the #1 player's only swap — a dominated class does not supply the meta's two best engines.
- Not SPLASH-ONLY: the splash evidence cuts the other way on inspection. Both champions pay real costs to rent Archer cards (12 deck slots; double-cost Hail). A native hero pays neither. Splash-only would be the verdict if the rentals were *free*; they are the most expensive rentals in the corpus, and they still win. That is evidence the native version is untested upside, not evidence the hero is correctly unplayed.
- UNBUILT: the class has a champion-grade engine (Post flood), the game's cheapest sweeper, its only direct burn, and a unique trade geometry — and has never once been fielded by a functioning policy, hosted or (since Round 1) locally.

### Shell sketch (for a future Round; not built in this pass)

- **Hero: Archer.** Native core at printed cost: Post ×4, Sniper ×4, Sharpshooter ×4, Hail of Arrows ×4, Bolt ×4 (20 cards).
- **The open problem is card velocity** — the Round must take a position on it:
  - *Arm A (inverted C2):* rent Mage draw the way macro rents Archer — "Mage" class trinkets ×~8 + Study ×4 + Plan ×4. The flood gets its refill; the tax is paid in the other direction.
  - *Arm B (pure native):* no draw; Post copies as the only velocity, Reload ×3 as pseudo-velocity (re-firing Sharpshooter pings / Post triggers — Reload's exact repeat semantics are unverified and must be read in sim.nim before this arm is designed around it), Ambusher ×4 as the toughness-2 body.
- **Excluded by evidence**: Wildfire (self-harm against a toughness-1 board — the v13 Burnout lesson, read the card) except possibly as a separate Gift-donate experiment, labeled speculative. Overcharge at most ×2.
- **Disciplines that transfer verbatim from the corpus**: macro's Post discipline (Post only with a minion in hand; minions held for a live Post), Sharpshooter pings to heroes, and a *re-derived* Hail gate for the 3-cost price (the 6-cost timing evidence does not transfer).
- **Mechanism the Round would test**: bodies/game + Post-copy conversion + ping/burn damage share of the *native-cost* engine vs macro-replica's rented engine in the same rooms; hand-at-death and unspent-energy profile (the predicted stall); death rate to opposing Hail (does the 1-toughness board die at the precon's implied rate, or does ranged/hidden/Bolt pressure change the race?).
- **Falsifier**: if the native engine, with velocity solved either way, still cannot beat the advancing line's league-shaped mark — or dies to Hail at stock-ref rates — the verdict revises toward DEAD CLASS and the class closes with evidence instead of absence.

### Unverified items (labeled, not load-bearing)
- daf's deck failure in the 79 untraced episodes (inferred from fixed policy version).
- Bolt bypassing Bubble (printed-rules reading of `attacked(You)`; never observed in play — no Archer hero has played).
- Gift→Wildfire donate combo (printed-rules reading; Gift appears in zero decks in the corpus, including the precon).

> **CORRECTION — 2026-11-26 (EE ~994).** "Gift appears in zero decks"
> is wrong. **Gift ×4 is carried by A-aron:v142 and salvo:v1** (536
> seat-games), cast **245 times**, and every cast donates exactly one
> Burnout clock to another hero (class-interactions.md §3b, §6 item
> 4). What stands: Gift is absent from the daf precon (the ×0 in this
> audit's pool table is correct). The correction strengthens this
> audit's UNBUILT verdict — Gift is a fourth Archer card with a
> proven, field-grade use — and it is the direct source of the class
> study's synthesis S1 (Clock Export). (Mistake register M16.)
- Reload's repeat semantics on triggered trinkets (Post) — must be settled from sim.nim before Arm B is built.
Study inscription (Stage 5, Round 41 close, EE 997-1000): the pairwise class-interaction matrix and synthesized candidates, inscribed verbatim from top2/class-interactions.md.

# Class-Interaction Study — Cards and Casters (Warrior × Mage × Archer)

**Status: ready-for-inscription** (continuous-training loop: please inscribe at the next free coordinate; this file is the study of record until then).
Date: 2026-11-26, ~EE 896, on Will's direction: "study the interactions between each class type, synthesize."
Sources: the 507 fully-traced hosted episodes (`traces-all/`, per-tick hashes validated in Rounds 29/33), `index.json` + `index_incremental_20261125.json` (policy/score per seat), `baseset.nim` as printed, the weakness register (W1–W10), the Archer audit (EE 894), and local screen history (Rounds 28–39). Analysis scripts: `interactions.py`, `interactions_diag.py`, `interactions_diag2.py` in this directory; raw aggregates in `interactions.json`. No candidate built or screened; no fleet runs — all computation is local trace analysis (three passes, ~30 s total wall).

---

## 0. The frame the matrix lives in

- **Every traced game is 5-player.** Field sizes in `traces-all/`: 5 seats × 507 games, no exceptions. All composition analysis below is therefore own-class-count analysis at fixed field size 5 (see Corrections §6.1).
- **Seats: 2,535 total — Warrior 1,221 / Mage 1,043 / Archer 271.** Played seats: 2,264 (Warrior + Mage only). All 271 Archer seats are daf-awm-v1:v2's empty deck, dead at tick 1 (Archer audit §0). The old "2,264 seat-slots" figure was the *played* count mislabeled as the total.
- Heroes are mechanically identical; class is a card economy (off-class +3, −1 per class trinket of that card's class in play). Every interaction below is therefore a card-pool interaction, priced in energy, deck slots, or both.
- Champion families in the corpus: rado (Warrior) 345 games, share 0.394; macroMage 236, 0.403; macroWarrior 109, 0.284. Everyone else ("field"): 1,574 played seats, 0.156.

---

## 1. The pairwise class-interaction matrix

### 1a. Win share by opposing class present (all played seats; source: traces-all + index, this study)

| Focal ↓ / Opponent → | Warrior present | Mage present | Archer present |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Warrior** (n=1,221; base 0.239) | — (mirror §1b) | 0.239 (n=1,180) vs 0.244 absent (n=41) — **flat** | 0.258 (n=594) vs 0.222 absent (n=627) — **+3.6, dead-seat artifact §1d** |
| **Mage** (n=1,043; base 0.206) | 0.206 (n=1,039) vs 0.250 absent (n=4, unusable) | — (mirror §1b) | 0.241 (n=490) vs 0.175 absent (n=553) — **+6.6, dead-seat artifact §1d** |
| **Archer** | **EMPTY** — never played | **EMPTY** — never played | **EMPTY** — never played |

Presence is too coarse for the two played classes (a class is present in ~97% of games). The signal is in the counts:

### 1b. Win share by NUMBER of opposing seats of each class (source: this study)

| Focal | vs 1 Warrior | vs 2 Warriors | vs 3+ Warriors | vs 1 Mage | vs 2 Mages | vs 3+ Mages |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Warrior** | 0.253 (458) | 0.244 (525) | 0.204 (181) | 0.245 (383) | 0.234 (552) | 0.241 (245) |
| **Mage** | 0.251 (183) | 0.205 (552) | 0.181 (304) | 0.218 (458) | 0.197 (417) | 0.212 (52) |

**The Warrior–Mage interaction is one-directional.** Each additional Warrior at the table taxes every Mage seat (~−3.5 pts per Warrior step, monotone 0.251 → 0.205 → 0.181); additional Mages do not tax Warriors at all (0.245 / 0.234 / 0.241, flat). Warrior density also taxes Warriors themselves at 3+ (0.204 — mirror crowding), while Mage mirrors are free (0.218 / 0.197 / 0.212, no trend).

Champion families, same instrument (source: this study):

| Family | vs 1 W | vs 2 W | vs 3+ W | vs 1 M | vs 2 M | vs 3+ M |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| rado (W) | 0.414 (152) | 0.410 (134) | 0.310 (42) | 0.396 (96) | 0.399 (163) | 0.372 (78) |
| macroMage | **0.509 (53)** | 0.386 (132) | **0.320 (50)** | 0.442 (95) | 0.381 (113) | 0.538 (13, small-n) |
| macroWarrior | 0.333 (27) | 0.278 (54) | 0.222 (27) | 0.283 (46) | 0.306 (49) | 0.400 (5, small-n) |

The gradient steepens at champion strength: macroMage loses **~19 points** from a 1-Warrior room to a 3+-Warrior room. rado is indifferent to Mage count and taxed only by Warrior crowding at 3+ (his mirror problem, W5, restated as a count term). This is consistent with W5's deck-signature cells (macroMage 0.313 vs radoDeck) — the signature was a proxy for the class count.

### 1c. Killer matrix — who kills whom (losses only; killer = seat of the action carrying the DefeatEvent, the register's method; source: this study)

| Victim ↓ / Killer → | Warrior | Mage | SELF | Median death tick by killer |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Warrior** (929 losses) | 388 (41.8%) | **530 (57.0%)** | 11 (1.2%) — **11/11 donated Burnout clocks** (§3b) | W 95.0 / M 93.5 / SELF 90 |
| **Mage** (828 losses) | **433 (52.3%)** | 368 (44.4%) | 27 (3.3%) — Burnout clocks, own or donated (§3b) | W 100 / M 92 / SELF 96 |
| **Archer** | **EMPTY** | **EMPTY** | **EMPTY** | — |

Kill source: minion attacks decide 96.4% of Warrior losses and 90.9% of Mage losses; spell-play kills are rare (13 and 12); trigger kills are a Mage-victim phenomenon (36, the Burnout economy). Cross-kills dominate both directions — each class is killed more often by the other class than by its own — but the tempo is asymmetric in the mirror: Mage kills Mage at median tick 92, the fastest cell in the matrix, while Warrior kills Mage latest (100). Winner class in the victim's losses (for completeness): Warrior losses → Warrior winner 484 / Mage 445; Mage losses → Warrior 531 / Mage 297.

### 1d. The only hosted "Archer interaction" that exists: the dead seat's price

daf's empty seat converts a 5-player game into a 4-player game with a free elimination. Measured value of that artifact, by focal seat (source: this study):

| Focal | With dead Archer seat | Without | Δ |
|---|---|---|---|
| Warrior seats | 0.258 (594) | 0.222 (627) | +3.6 |
| Mage seats | 0.241 (490) | 0.175 (553) | +6.6 |
| rado | 0.425 (174) | 0.363 (171) | +6.2 |
| macroMage | **0.469 (113)** | **0.341 (123)** | **+12.8** |
| macroWarrior | 0.300 (60) | 0.265 (49) | +3.5 |

This is **not** evidence about a played Archer — it is the price of a missing opponent, and it is largest for the flood (one fewer Hail deck and one fewer focus voter against the widest board). Two consequences: (i) every historical "vs Archer" reading in the programme is this artifact; (ii) hosted reads taken in daf-containing fields — including parts of v30's first hosted series — are inflated by a field-composition term that has nothing to do with the candidate.

### 1e. Archer cells priced from local instruments (no hosted data exists)

| Cell | Local price | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Archer hero, any field | **Unmeasured as a build.** Stock precon + generic logic is the weakest gauntlet seat: pooled ~2.7% vs stock Warrior ref ~7.8% — a precon measurement, not a class measurement | Gauntlet Table G/GD, Rounds 28–36; Archer audit §3a |
| Archer engine rented (Mage hero) | Win share 0.403 at champion strength (macroMage, §2) — the engine's value is proven at the top of the meta, at a 12-slot tax | Corpus, this study |
| Archer sweeper rented (Warrior hero) | Hail at 6 energy is the #1 player's only deck deviation; his fire band is §3a | Corpus; W2 as corrected §6.2 |
| Archer burn (Bolt) | 11 casts in 507 games, all rented (macro C3), 22 hero damage, exactly 2.00/cast, zero observed interference — native Bolt has never been cast | This study §3d |

---

## 2. Rental flows — every cross-class flow in the corpus is a measured interaction

Census over all 2,264 played seat-games (source: setup decks, this study). A "flow" is off-class copies in a deck; the tax is paid in energy (+3/card, discounted per trinket in play) and/or deck slots (class trinkets).

| Flow | Seats carrying | Copies | Per carrier | Trinket tax | What is being bought |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Mage → Archer** | 772 / 1,043 Mage seats (74%) | 10,424 | 13.5 cards | **10–12 slots** ("Archer" ×10–12; mean off-class trinket slots across all Mage seats 8.28) | The Post flood engine + Gift (§3b) |
| **Warrior → Archer** | 594 / 1,221 Warrior seats (49%) | 2,376 | 4.0 (Hail ×4, always exactly) | **0 slots** — tax paid in energy: Hail at 6 vs 3 native | The sweeper |
| **Warrior → Mage** | 81 / 1,221 (7%) | 324 | 4.0 (Bubble ×4) | 0 slots — Bubble at 3 (0+3) | Attack insurance (macroWarrior C1/C4 only) |
| **Archer → anything** | **0** | 0 | — | — | **EMPTY — never played** |
| **anyone → Warrior cards** | **0** | 0 | — | — | **Warrior's pool has zero export demand in 2,264 seat-games** |
| Own-class trinkets | 0 seats | 0 | — | — | Trinkets exist in this meta *only* as rental instruments |

The Mage → Archer flow, by deck (the complete list of off-class decks in the corpus is nine; these are the Mage ones):

| Policy | Hero | Trinkets | Archer cards rented | Games | Share |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| cac-macromackie:v64 (C2) | Mage | "Archer" ×12 | Post ×4, Sharpshooter ×4, Sniper ×4 | 190 | (family 0.403) |
| cac-macromackie:v64 (C3) | Mage | "Archer" ×10 | Post ×4, Sharpshooter ×4, Sniper ×4, **Bolt ×2** | 46 | (family 0.403) |
| A-aron:v142 | Mage | "Archer" ×10 | Post ×4, Sharpshooter ×4, **Gift ×4** | 269 | 0.203 (state.md) |
| salvo:v1 | Mage | "Archer" ×12 | Post ×4, Sharpshooter ×4, Sniper ×4, **Gift ×4** | 267 | 0.170 (state.md) |

**What the flows price.** (i) The Post engine is worth 12 of 40 deck slots plus a permanent surcharge risk to the #2 player — the largest rental in the corpus, paid by three independent policies (macro, A-aron, salvo), i.e. the Mage field's consensus, not one player's taste. (ii) The discount is per trinket *in play*, and the trinkets are 0-cost permanents: a renter's effective cost on Archer cards converges toward native as its trinket board builds — the tax is paid in *slots and early turns*, not steady-state energy. A native Archer hero pays neither, ever. (iii) Hail's rental clears at double energy with zero slots from three Warrior policies (radogost 345 g, cac-rado:v1 204 g, orch 45 g) — the sweeper is worth 6; nobody has priced it at 3. (iv) Warrior cards are never rented: Bear/Footsoldier bodies are commodities the other classes generate better (Post copies, Banner/Footsoldier summons are Warrior-internal), and Warrior's spells are board-bound. The class with the most seats exports nothing.

Our own line's flows, for the record: v30/v33 (Mage hero) rent the C2 shell verbatim — 12 trinket slots, Post/Sharpshooter/Sniper — and rent **no Gift**; the Warrior line (v6–v22) rents Hail ×4 at 6, the rado pattern.

---

## 3. Mechanical interactions

### 3a. Hail's 1-tough band vs each class's board

Board toughness profile, measured over every pre-action state in the corpus (minion instances; source: this study): **Warrior boards are 11.6% toughness-≤1 (47,997 / 414,903 instances); Mage boards are 86.8% (116,857 / 134,642)** — the flood's copied bodies are printed at 1 and Warrior's are printed at ≥2 (Bear 3/2, Footsoldier 1/2, Tactician 1/2, Commander 2/3), so the band is a *class* property before it is a damage property. Hail fires in the corpus: **242, all by Warrior heroes** (no other class carries it). At fire time: opposing minions median **12**, of which toughness-≤1 median **4** (current-toughness and printed-toughness definitions agree; in macroMage rooms, 96 fires, same median 4). So the rented Hail is priced and fired as a *width* answer whose kill band covers ~1/3 of a typical opposing field and ~0% of an undamaged Warrior board — it is, mechanically, a Mage-board answer carried by Warrior heroes. An Archer hero's Hail at 3 energy fires profitably into bands the 6-energy version must wait out; that earlier window has never existed in a game. (W2's fire-band numbers are corrected in §6.2.)

### 3b. The Burnout economy — clocks minted, started by opponents, and donated

Burnout (Mage card, summoned when Overwork is destroyed: 2 damage + a toss at each of its owner's end-of-turns) is the corpus's richest cross-class object. Full accounting (source: this study, diag passes):

- **770 Burnout summons** in 507 games; 100% land on the Overwork owner's board.
- **283 of 770 (36.8%) were started by an opposing Warrior's Pillage** destroying the Overwork — the Warrior class's trinket-destruction spell routinely converts a Mage's live engine (extra energy + draw) into a self-clock on the Mage, at the Warrior's chosen moment.
- **245 Gift casts** (salvo 101, A-aron 144 — the only Gift carriers) and **245 Burnout GainControl events: every Gift cast in the corpus donates exactly one Burnout clock** to another hero. Recipients include 131 Warrior seat-games that carried a donated clock (131/131 arrived via GainControl in a game with a Gift cast).
- The killer matrix's SELF row is this economy settling: Mage victims 27 self-kills, Warrior victims 11 — and **11/11 Warrior self-kills are donated-clock ticks** (TriggerEvent Burnout, damage 2, on the victim's own turn boundary). A Warrior hero has no Burnout in its deck; every Warrior who "killed himself" was killed by a Mage card a Mage gave him.
- macro does **not** donate (no Gift in C1–C5) — he eats his own clock: W4's 11 losses (7.8% of macroMage) ending on his own Burnout at life 1–4. A-aron and salvo export theirs. Our v30/v33 eat ours too: 30 of 247 losses (12.1%) in the Round 38 autopsy were self-Burnout deaths — the advancing line currently plays the macro side of this asymmetry, not the field's.

Removal asymmetry: a Mage hero has **no native answer** to a donated (or own) Burnout — Oozification and Bounce target minions, Control steals minions. The only exits are exporting it (Gift) or dying on schedule. A Warrior hero holds a printed answer: Pillage destroys *a* trinket (no owner restriction as printed — **printed-rules reading, unverified in play**), including, on that reading, a donated Burnout on its own board, and gains the +1/+0 or +0/+1 branch for doing it.

### 3c. Draw economy — the real class gap

DrawEvents per seat-game (source: this study): **Warrior 5.98** (median 6 — statistically indistinguishable from the bare turn draw over a ~30-turn game; Warrior's pool has no draw and rents none) vs **Mage 10.89** (median 10; ~+4.9 extra draws/game from Plan/Study/Overwork). At death: Warrior heroes die with **29.4 cards still in deck** (of 35 post-opening) vs Mage 25.0; hand at death is equal (~4.3 both). A Warrior game is played from roughly the first 11 cards of the deck; the class's losses are not hand-starvation at death (4.37 cards in hand) but *selection* starvation — it never sees the rest of its deck. This is the quantitative form of Archer's hole (zero native draw, audit §2) and of why every successful Archer-card strategy in the corpus is rented by the class that can draw.

### 3d. Bolt vs life curves

Bolt (Archer, 1 energy, 2 to a hero — the set's only direct burn): **11 casts in 507 games**, all by macro's C3 (Mage hero, rented at 2 copies), producing 22 same-action hero damage — exactly 2.00 per cast, no observed bounce/interference (consistent with the audit's printed-rules reading that Bubble answers attacks, not spell damage; now observed 11/11, small-n). Bolt has never been cast by an Archer hero, never at native cost, and never in the endgame band where W1 says champions leak lethal — the 8-damage-for-4-energy closing package (×4) is entirely unpriced in play.

### 3e. Post-copy conversion by hero class

SummonEvents of Archer minions (Post copies; no other card summons them) per Post play and per game (source: this study):

| Hero / policy | Post plays | Copies | Copies/Post play | Copies/game |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Mage — macro (C2/C3) | 338 (236 g) | 1,001 | **2.97** | **4.24** |
| Mage — salvo | 286 (267 g) | 667 | 2.33 | 2.50 |
| Mage — A-aron | 276 (269 g) | 583 | 2.11 | 2.17 |
| Mage — all seats | 900 (1,043 g) | 2,251 | 2.50 | 2.16 |
| Warrior heroes | 0 | 0 | — | 0 (Post never carried) |
| Archer hero | **EMPTY** | **EMPTY** | — | — |

Conversion is a *pilot* stat as much as a class stat (macro converts 40% better than the other renters on the same engine — his Post-with-minion discipline, 337/337 in the register's count). But every conversion number in existence was produced under the rental: 10–12 slots spent, surcharge live until the trinket board builds. The native cell — same engine, no tax, 12 slots returned to the deck — is the largest EMPTY cell in the study.

### 3f. Ranged vs attack economies

Printed mechanics only (no new trace measurement claimed): Sniper/Sharpshooter attack non-ranged minions and take no combat damage back (sim.nim, per the audit) — against Warrior's ≥2-toughness bodies a 2–3-power ranged body removes them over successive turns at zero body cost, a grinding geometry neither other class replicates (Warrior trades bodies; Mage pays cards — Bounce/Oozification/Control). The corpus's one ranged measurement is defensive: macro's Sharpshooter pings go to heroes 1,052/0, and W9 priced the creature-sparing at ~nothing (the spared creatures die to the field). The offensive half — ranged bodies as repeatable, retaliation-free removal against Warrior boards — has never been fielded by anyone, because the only ranged bodies in the meta are rented flood tokens that die to the first Hail.

---

## 4. Field-composition effects

- **Field size is constant (5)** in the traced corpus; all terms below are count terms at size 5.
- **Warrior count** is the master composition variable: it taxes Mage seats monotonically (§1b), taxes Warrior seats at 3+ (mirror crowding), and is the axis along which macroMage swings ±19 points. **Mage count taxes nobody** — flat for Warrior focals, flat in the Mage mirror. **Archer count** is the dead-seat artifact (§1d) until someone plays the class.
- **W7 (class lottery) reframed as an interaction term:** macro's hero-class draw (Mage 0.403 / Warrior 0.284, Δ = 11.9 pts, n = 236/109) is a *self*-interaction — the same policy, the same card pool rented two ways — and it is larger than every field-composition term in this study except the dead seat's gift to macroMage (+12.8). Any bar priced off macro's blended 0.327 is pricing his draw, not his strength (the register's W7 conclusion, now with the full interaction table behind it).
- **W8 (seat position) reframed:** on the full corpus rado's 5p shares by seat are 0.458 / 0.397 / 0.361 / 0.333 / **0.403** (n = 83/68/72/60/62) — a seats-2–3 dip with seat 4 recovering, not a monotone late-seat penalty (corrected §6.3). Seat is a small term next to class counts.
- **Practical consequence for our screens:** league-shaped tables for a Mage candidate should be judged against the Warrior-count mix of the actual league field, not a blended field — a candidate screened in 1-Warrior rooms is being priced ~19 points above its 3+-Warrior price at champion strength.

---

## 5. Synthesis — candidate sketches, ranked by (unpriced asymmetry × cheapness of the decisive test)

Sketches only. No builds, no screens were run for this study.

### S1 — Clock Export (Mage line: v33 + Gift) — ranked first
- **Hero class:** Mage (the advancing line). **Engine:** the C2 shell we already rent — 12 "Archer" trinkets are *already in the deck*, so Gift's rental tax is already paid; the only cost is 2–4 deck slots.
- **Interaction exploited:** §3b's Burnout asymmetry. The field's other Mage policies export their clocks (245 donations); we eat ours (30/247 losses, 12.1%, Round 38 autopsy — worse than macro's 7.8%, and macro at least has no Gift because his shell predates the lesson). Gift converts our single largest self-inflicted death class into a weapon aimed at the class that receives donations worst (Warrior heroes, who die on donated clocks and hold the only removal — which costs them their Pillage).
- **Mechanism metric:** self-Burnout deaths in the traced arm vs the v33 control arm (autopsy machinery already counts exactly this); Gift casts/game; donated-clock damage credited.
- **Falsifier:** casts < 0.5/game in traced arms (the slots are dead), or self-Burnout deaths do not fall against the same-plan control — the export premise is wrong and the slots return.

### S2 — Archer-native flood, pure native (the audit's Arm B)
- **Hero class:** Archer. **Engine:** native Post/Sniper/Sharpshooter/Hail/Bolt at printed cost; velocity from Post copies + Reload as pseudo-velocity (Reload's repeat semantics on Post to be settled from sim.nim first — the audit's flagged unknown).
- **Interaction exploited:** §2/§3e — the meta's best engine, rented by everyone at 10–12 slots, run natively with the slots returned; plus §3a's Hail at 3 (fires into the median-4 band profitably instead of waiting for width 12) and §3d's Bolt close (8 damage for 4 energy against the W1 endgame leak).
- **Mechanism metric:** copies/game and bodies/game vs the rented benchmarks (hosted macro 4.24 copies/g; replica-Mage in-room 6.67 copies / 23.3 bodies); hand-at-death and unspent-energy stall profile (the predicted failure); death rate to opposing Hail.
- **Falsifier:** (the audit's) native engine with velocity addressed still cannot beat the advancing line's league-shaped mark, or dies to Hail at stock-ref rates → verdict revises toward DEAD CLASS, closed with evidence.

### S3 — Inverted C2 (the audit's Arm A: Archer hero renting Mage draw)
- **Hero class:** Archer. **Engine:** native flood + rented Mage draw ("Mage" trinkets ~×8, Study ×4, Plan ×4) — macro's rental run in the opposite direction.
- **Interaction exploited:** §3c — draw is the real class gap (10.89 vs ~6.0); this sketch buys exactly the missing stat and nothing else, keeping the native engine and native Hail/Bolt.
- **Mechanism metric:** draws/game ≥ 9 *and* copies/game ≥ 4.24 (the rented benchmark) in the same traced arms — both halves must bind; if draw binds and conversion doesn't, the native-Post premise itself fails.
- **Falsifier:** draws solved but conversion below the rented benchmark → the engine's value was the Mage shell around it, not the cards; the class revises toward SPLASH-ONLY. Ranked below S2 because a failure cannot be attributed to one half.

### S4 — Pillage clock discipline (Warrior line: v22 descendant)
- **Hero class:** Warrior. **Engine:** stock Warrior + Hail rental (the existing line), one targeting hunk: Pillage.
- **Interaction exploited:** §3b both directions — (i) *start* clocks deliberately: prefer Pillage on a live Overwork when its owner is entering the W4 band (life ≤ ~8 and falling), converting their engine at the moment the clock hurts most, instead of the incidental 36.8% the field already gets; (ii) *refuse* clocks: Pillage our own donated Burnout before its first tick (printed-rules reading — Pillage's target has no owner restriction as printed; **unverified in play**, settle in the simulator before the screen, not during).
- **Mechanism metric:** Pillage-on-Overwork share of Pillage plays; donated-clock ticks taken before removal; target's death-by-own-Burnout rate in traced arms.
- **Falsifier:** either hunk fires < 1 per 5 games in replica rooms → the interaction is incidental at our strength and the discipline closes. Ranked fourth: the asymmetry is real but smaller, and the Warrior line is not the advancing line.

---

## 6. Corrections to the inherited record (this study's recounts)

1. **Weakness register, corpus frame:** "fields are 4–5 players" — the traced corpus is **507/507 five-player**; and its header ("NO Archer-class player exists in the corpus (2,264 seat-slots: Warrior 1,221, Mage 1,043)") predates the Archer audit and should be updated to the audit's finding: **2,535 seats; 271 Archer seats, all daf's empty deck, never played**. (The 630-row untraced index may contain other field sizes; the traces are unanimous.)
2. **W2's Hail fire-band distribution does not reproduce.** Register: rado's fires at enemy 1-tough counts "min 3, p25 9, median 11." On the validated traces (all 242 Warrior-hero fires; 96 in macroMage rooms): **1-tough median 4** under both current- and printed-toughness definitions; the ~11–12 figure is the median *total* opposing minions. W2's qualitative claim survives weakened (Hail prices 1-tough kills; an undamaged Warrior board is immune — §3a's 11.6% vs 86.8% board shares support the immunity half directly), but its band numbers overstate the 1-tough mass ~3× and should not be used to set a Hail gate. W2's died-holding-Hail count (122/209) was not re-measured here and is untouched.
3. **W8's seat splits do not reproduce on the full corpus.** Register: seats 0–1 0.456 (36/79), seat 4 0.345 (10/29). Full-corpus recount: **0.458 / 0.397 / 0.361 / 0.333 / 0.403 (n = 83/68/72/60/62)** — the penalty is a seats-2–3 dip; seat 4 recovers. Direction survives at reduced size; the "late-seat" framing does not.
4. **Archer audit, Gift:** its statement that Gift "appears in zero decks in the corpus" is **wrong** (its precon column, ×0, is correct). Gift ×4 is carried by A-aron:v142 and salvo:v1 (536 seat-games), cast 245 times, and every cast donates a Burnout (§3b). This materially strengthens the audit's own UNBUILT verdict — a fourth Archer card with a proven, field-grade use — and it is the direct source of sketch S1.
5. **This study's brief** described fields of "3–7 seats": not in the traced corpus (all 5). Composition claims here are count-at-size-5 claims.

## 7. What this study did not do

No builds, no screens, no fleet runs, no hosted queries. Ranged (§3f) and the Pillage-on-own-Burnout reading (S4) rest on printed rules and are labeled as such. Reload's repeat semantics remain unsettled (the audit's flag stands). All Archer hero cells are EMPTY in hosted data and priced only from the local instruments in §1e — the sketches are ranked so the cheapest decisive test (S1) runs on the advancing line first, and the class question (S2/S3) is settled by a build the loop can schedule, not by further corpus archaeology: the corpus has now said everything it knows about Archer, which is that nobody has played it.

*End of study. Companion data: `interactions.json`; scripts: `interactions.py`, `interactions_diag.py`, `interactions_diag2.py`.*
