### Estimates Will asked for (EE ~915)

- **How long did it take the competitors to accumulate their counts?**
  radogost: **43.9 days** (Oct 1 → Nov 14) to v166 — but the shape is
  burst/tail/sprint, not steady: ~30 versions in the first ~8 hours,
  ~2.3/day through October, 42 in the final 60 hours. macromackie:
  **3.8 days** (Nov 10–14) to v64 — a single sprint at 17/day, and he
  submitted every upload. A-aron: **not resolvable publicly** — uploads
  carry no timestamps; bounds are: v142 existed by 2026-11-11T15:59Z,
  his first submission was 2026-11-10T08:13Z, so IF uploads began with
  his league entry the 142 accumulated in ≤1.3 days (≥89/day — an
  automated bulk-iteration signature); the start could be earlier and
  the true span longer. stilt: 43.9 days to v30 (~0.7/day).
- **Time for us to "hit v170"** (one policy accumulating 170 versions):
  at radogost's lifetime average (3.8/day) ≈ **45 days**; at
  macromackie's sprint rate (17/day) ≈ **10 days**; at our own local
  build rate (33 builds in 3 days ≈ 11/day, if every build were
  uploaded to a single policy) ≈ **15–16 days**. At our actual
  upload behaviour (upload only to submit, only on Will's word; 3
  uploads in 2.3 days) the counter never accumulates — our version
  stock is a naming choice, not a throughput limit.
- **What a publish EVENT looks like here**: a submission instantly
  supersedes the submitter's seated champion (auto_champion: always),
  appears in S5 with the submitter's title/description/notes, and the
  leaderboard's version_rounds_played resets. After 12 frozen days, any
  top-5 publish is a league-visible event — and the historical trigger
  pattern is clustering: the whole field's last publishes landed within
  the same ~5 days (Nov 10–14), i.e. publishes have historically come
  in field-wide bursts around the league's opening phase, not as
  individual responses we can observe.

## Per-competitor dossiers (publicly knowable)

- **Andre von Auto / radogost** (owner Andre H). Warrior, stock deck +
  Duel→Hail swap (from our traced corpus, S4). 101 submissions — the
  league's most prolific submitter, all before Nov 14. His early
  submissions carry no text; the design diary is in the corpus, not in
