  (truncated in our pull) and his one rule (hold Swords/Shields until a
  minion is controlled).
- **phext (us)**: three submissions (v6 2026-11-24, v16 2026-11-24,
  v30 2026-11-26), each a separate policy at v1; our v6/v16 submission
  descriptions are as public as everyone else's.

## Leak flags (exposure beyond what the platform seems to intend)

1. **Submission title/description/notes are served to every
   authenticated user** (S5). Submitters wrote them like changelogs —
   macromackie's narrate his design evolution and local gates;
   Eckstar's and Rohit's disclose local evaluation numbers and an exact
   build recipe. Whether or not the platform intends these as public,
   they are the single richest competitor-intelligence surface in the
   system. Flagged, not exploited beyond reading what the endpoint
   returns to any signed-in user.
2. **Owner real-name fragments on the anonymous leaderboard** (S1):
   owner_name ("Andre H", "Scott M", …) is in the anon-200 response.
3. **World-readable replay binaries** on public S3 (S4): access control
   rests entirely on the URL staying undisclosed; the API that
   discloses URLs requires auth, the files themselves do not.
4. **CORRECTED (2026-11-26, EE ~994) — per-seat agent logs are NOT
   public.** This item originally claimed any authenticated user
   could download any seat's agent logs; the field study refuted
   it (field.md §2): the manifest serves `player_logs` only for
   seats the requester owns, and the direct route returns 403
   "You do not own this policy" (verified against five rival
   policies). What remains true: complete behavioral traces of
   competitors' games ARE public — through replays and game logs
   (S4), not seat logs. "Private policy" protects the file; the
   play is public via replay; a seat's own logs are private to
   its owner. See the correction note at S4.

## The not-public boundary (evidence-based)

Policy FILES are not public: the league sets `public_player_files:
false` (S2), there is no policy-file download endpoint (policy and
policy-version detail routes 404, S6), and no competitor code exists
off-platform (S9). Unsubmitted uploads are invisible except as gaps in
submitted version numbers — their timestamps, contents, and count
beyond the final counter are not exposed (A-aron's 135). Private
xp-requests are excluded from the coworld episode listing (only
non-private episodes appear; a `private: true` flag exists in the
request contract). There are no player profiles, bios, join dates, or
cross-league histories (S6), no standings history (S10), and no forum
or wiki competitor content (S7). In one line: **everything a competitor
has DONE in public competition — every seat, score, trace, submission
and their own submission prose — is readable by any signed-in user
(much of it anonymously); everything they ARE PREPARING — files,
unsubmitted versions, private tests — is not.**
Study inscription (Stage 5, Round 41 close, EE 997-1000): the verified deployment capabilities, inscribed verbatim from top2/deployment-capabilities.md.

# Deployment capabilities — the hosted Cards and Casters policy runtime

Audit for Will's questions (EE ~918): *what capabilities are available in the
deployment? can you install a small phext engine to support learning on the fly?*

Every claim below cites its primary source. Anything not sourced is labeled
UNVERIFIED. Sources:

- **bots.nim** — `games/cac` bot integration, hub clone `~/polyworld` (copy at
  `~/workspace/cac-dist/top2/src/bots.nim`, 1,606 lines). This is the runtime
  contract: host functions, budgets, lifecycle.
- **bassy** — the BASIC interpreter, pinned in `~/polyworld/nimby.lock` to
  `github.com/treeform/bassy` commit `a9effca`; source read at that commit
  (`src/bassy.nim`, `src/bassy/numbers.nim`, `src/bassy/texts.nim`).
- **headless.nim / match.nim / sim.nim** — same tree as bots.nim; headless.nim's
  `when defined(coworld)` block is the hosted episode path itself.
- **manifest** — `coworld_manifest.json` for cow_dbe67c76 (player protocol text,
  variants, config schema), in the downloaded package at
  `~/workspace/softmax/softmax-player/coworld/…`.
- **play.md** — `games/cac/play.md` (bot-writing quick start).
- **onboarding** — `~/workspace/softmax/cards-onboarding.md` Step 3 (the game's
  own getting-started text).

