  clock cap), out of the genome's search space by name.

Total standing choices inventoried: 31 entries (D1–D31).
The KEEP set is the set the new constraints genuinely do
not touch — and none of it was kept by default: each KEEP
was re-read against the log, the ledger, introspection,
and setup before being left alone, and each says why in
its line.

---

# PART 2 — THE EVOLUTION LOOP

Rapid evolution on the phext-core substrate. The loop's unit
is the programme's unit: a generation is screened in the
EE 896 iteration unit (20 sub-minute sub-rounds), league-
shaped first, mechanism metrics from traces on the identical
seed plan. The loop exists because the lineage's own history
is the control experiment: 39 rounds of single-lineage
minimal diffs plateaued at ~12 points below the bar for
three consecutive rounds (R37–R39), each round's candidate
a one-hunk child of the last. The loop must not re-become
that: it runs a population, keeps diversity by rule, and
selects on pre-stated criteria — or it is just the old
programme with more bots.

## The genome

A genome is: one portfolio shell (D2) + one value for every
gene below + the libphext-bas substrate version it builds
on. Genes are the EVOLVE parameters of Part 1 plus the
θ0s of the GEN-0 thetas. Nothing else varies. In particular
the core (libphext.bas) is never a gene, the structural
rules (D13's Post discipline, D19's ping rule, D26's
retired cap) are never genes, and class is a portfolio
property (D1), not a gene.

| Gene | Values | Start | From |
|---|---|---|---|
| `gamma` (drift rate) | 0.75 / 0.875 / 0.9375 | 0.875 | core law |
| `clampLo/ clampHi` | (0.5, 2.0) / (0.67, 1.5) / (0.4, 2.5) | (0.5, 2.0) | core law |
| `relBand0` | 5 / 8 / 11 | 8 | D14 |
| `releaseN` | 6 / 7 / 8 | 7 | D14 |
| `burnBand0` | 3 / 4 / 6 | 4 | D16 |
| `focPair` (swing/score θ0) | 2/1 / 3/2 / 5/3 | 3/2 | D5 |
| `hailTau` (press threshold) | 0.15 / 0.25 / 0.40 | 0.25 | D5 τ table |
| `clockTau` (ease threshold) | 0.25 / 0.35 / 0.50 | 0.35 | D5 τ table |
| `wH` (focus hail-threat weight) | 0 / 2 / 4 | 2 | D4 |
| `aimKill / aimBase / aimPowW` | 8/3/1 … 12/6/3 (3 named triples) | 10/4/2 | D8 |
| `faceBase / killBase / chip` | (8,4,2) / (10,6,6) / (14,8,6) | (10,6,6) | D10 |
| `priPost / priOw / priDraw` | gaps: (80,60,20) / (80,70,30) / (90,85,45) | (80,70,30) | D12 |
| `owBand` | off / 6 / 8 / 10 | off | D15 |
| `keepDraw / keepEngine / keepBody` | 3 named triples each | current table | D20 |
| `tieEps` | 0 / 1 / 2 | 0 | D23 |
| `giftSwap` | 0 / 2 / 4 | 0 | D2/D28 |
| `hailSwap` | 0 / 2 | 0 | D2 |
| `bubbleN` | 3 / 4 | 4 | D2 |
| `owN` | 3 / 4 | 4 | D2 |
| `compRel / compFoc` | (0,0) / (2,1) / (4,2) | (2,1) | D29 |
| `hailGate / hailPost` (Warrior line) | 5/6/8 × off/on | 6/off | D25 |

Mutation: a child differs from its parent in **1–3 genes**
— enough to attribute, few enough to read. Crossover is
permitted only between the generation's two selected parents
and only at whole-gene granularity. Every variant's genome
is written in its bot header in the lineage's delta-note
form, so the file remains its own record.

## The generation cycle

Population: **12 variants** per generation (the roster
below for generation 1). One generation = one round of the
continuous loop = 20 sub-minute passes, spent by rule:

- **Passes 1–8 — the wide screen.** All 12 variants,
  league-shaped table only, shallow depth (each pass is
  one minute of fleet wall; passes rotate variants so
  every variant accumulates comparable depth). Read:
  league-shaped share + the generation's mechanism sanity
  metrics (θ-movement > 0 for stateful arms; shape clauses
  from R37 for the shell). Cut: the bottom half by
  league-shaped share, subject to the diversity rule.
- **Passes 9–16 — the deepening.** The surviving 6,
  league-shaped + CF3 tables, depth accumulating toward
  the per-table standard. Mechanism metrics computed from
  traces on the identical seed plan for all 6 (the
  generation's named metrics — see the roster: each
  variant carries the metric of the Part 1 entry it
  instantiates, and the falsifier travels with it).
- **Passes 17–20 — the final.** The top 3 by the selection
  rule get full per-table depth (21,200/table cumulative)
  + the gauntlet subject tables (within-class clause:
  Mage vs Mage binds) + full traces. The generation's
  verdict is read here, under the advance rule below.

**Selection rule (stated before any run, per generation):**
rank by (1) mechanism/shape pass — a variant whose named
mechanism metric falsifies is out regardless of share
(Round 34 doctrine: a share win with a failed shape test
is an unexplained residual, not an advance); (2)
league-shaped share at accumulated depth; (3) CF3 share
as the tiebreak. The top 2 become the next generation's
parents.

**Diversity rule (the anti-plateau clause):** the cut at
pass 8 and the parent selection both preserve **one variant
per deck-gene family** — the best `giftSwap > 0` variant,
the best `hailSwap > 0` variant, and the best pure-shell
variant each survive the wide cut if they are within noise
(≤ 1.5 points of league-shaped share) of the last
unprotected survivor, and at least one parent slot goes to
a non-pure family whenever such a variant reaches the
final. Rationale, from the record: the lineage's plateau
was a diversity failure before it was an ideas failure —
every round's candidate was the previous round's child.
A loop that lets one family eat the population in
generation 1 has re-become the lineage by generation 3.

**Warrior-line generations** alternate in only when a
Part 1 Warrior entry is the named target (D25's gate is
the first): a Warrior generation's population is built on
v22 + the ledger gate, judged within-class against v22's
bar (gauntlet pooled 9.8%, clause binding in full).

## The Gate 1 pre-statement for stateful candidates
(standing — every generation inherits this text)

> These candidates are stateful: their parameters drift
> within the episode as a deterministic function of the
> observed event stream. Paired-seed divergence against
> the parent is therefore expected to start near zero and
> **grow with episode length** as the thetas separate.
> That growth IS the mechanism, not noise. The gate reads
> as follows for every stateful candidate:
> (1) **Determinism first:** a duplicate-seed pair must
> produce identical games (identical winner, identical
> traced hash sequence). Any mismatch is a hard fail —
> the learner has touched an unseeded entropy source.
> (2) **SIGHTED, inverted:** divergence ≥ 1% over the
> ≥ 2,000 paired cells at full-episode depth stamps the
> candidate SIGHTED — the learner demonstrably changes
> decisions. Divergence **< 1% at full depth is a
> REJECTION, not a pass**: for a stateful candidate it
> means the learning never engaged (the θ-movement
> metric must corroborate: median |θ − θ0| at episode
> end > 0 in the traced arm). The stateless reading of
> the gate (screen-blind below 1%) does not apply and
> must not be quoted for these candidates.
> (3) Divergence is reported with the median episode
> length of the probe, since the reading is
> length-dependent by construction.
> Probe scripts are written fresh per generation (never
> sed-derived); dispatches sequential per candidate per
> node — the standing Gate 1 machinery discipline,
> unchanged.

## When a generation's winner becomes the advancing line

Under the amended advance rule (gauntlet.txt, ruling (c)),
unchanged: the generation winner advances iff **(a)** its
mechanism/shape test passed (the generation's pre-stated
metrics, falsifiers armed), **(b)** its league-shaped share
beats the advancing line's same-round league-shaped mark,
and **(c)** for a within-class winner (Mage vs the Mage
line — the normal case), its gauntlet pooled subject value
does not bleed beyond the generation's stated allowance
(1 point, the R37 precedent) against the advancing line's
same-round pooled value. A cross-class finalist (an
Archer-native generation, when S2/S3 enter the loop) is
judged on (a) + (b) + CF/CF3, its gauntlet value measured,
recorded, and priced, not blocking. Advancement is a local
line decision; the seated champion (v30) is untouched by
it — succession is Will's word only.

## When evolution yields back to hand design

The loop stops and a hand round is designed instead when
any of these fires — each is a named plateau/falsity
signature, not a mood:

1. **Two consecutive generations** fail to beat the
   advancing line's league-shaped mark by more than noise
   (the R37–R39 plateau signature, now with a tripwire
   instead of a third repetition).
2. **A substrate falsifier fires:** θ-movement ≈ 0 across
   a generation's traces (the learner is inert — hand-fix
   the core in libphext-bas before any further generation),
   or a mechanism metric falsifies a REPLACE entry's core
   assumption (e.g., D15's falsifier: clock deaths
   unpriceable at the play decision).
3. **A hosted receipt contradicts the local model at
   scale** — the R33 event (replica floors shown soft;
   local gains withdrawn). Evolution on a broken
   instrument manufactures progress; the instruments get
   a hand refresh first.
4. **A new core guidance lands from Will.** Preemption
   (EE ~922) outranks the loop: the generation in flight
   parks at its current pass, recorded, and the redesign
   is re-read under the new guidance before the next
   generation is rosters.

## Generation 1 — the roster

Base: **v36-phextcore** (generation 0: v33 + libphext.bas
core; thetas relBand 8, focSwingB/focScoreB 3/2, burnBand
4; γ 0.875; clamps [0.5, 2.0]; all else v33 verbatim).
Every variant below is v36 + the named gene values — and
for the three structural variants, the named Part 1
REPLACE implemented at its minimal form (the loop builds
them as candidates under the normal discipline; this
document specifies, it does not build).

1. **G1-01 CONTROL** — v36 verbatim. The baseline every
   variant is priced against; also carries gen-0's marks
   forward so generation 1 reads against generation 0,
   not against memory.
2. **G1-02 FASTDRIFT** — `gamma` 0.75 (effective memory
   ~4 observations). Metric: θ-movement magnitude vs
   control; falsifier per D5 (movement without share
   delta = τ table wrong).
3. **G1-03 SLOWDRIFT** — `gamma` 0.9375 (memory ~16).
   The γ bracket with G1-02: the drift rate is the
   learner's core constant and is priced first, before
   any knob it moves.
4. **G1-04 WIDEENV** — clamps (0.4, 2.5). Tests whether
   the [0.5, 2] envelope is the binding constraint on
   adaptation.
5. **G1-05 NARROWENV** — clamps (0.67, 1.5). The other
   bracket; if both brackets lose to control, the
   envelope is right and leaves the genome.
6. **G1-06 EARLYRELEASE** — `relBand0` 11, `releaseN` 6.
   D14's genes pushed toward survival: the wall is
   champion survival, and this is the roster's direct
   shot at it. Metric: held-at-death rate, death turn.
7. **G1-07 HOLDFAST** — `relBand0` 5, `releaseN` 8. The
   opposite bracket: trust the flood discipline longer.
   G1-06/G1-07 together price the release axis in one
   generation instead of two hand rounds (the v34/v39
   pattern, retired).
8. **G1-08 TICKPARAM** — `owBand` 8 (D15 implemented:
   ledger count × band Overwork pricing; v35's parked
   mechanism, tested as a gene inside the loop).
   Metric: self-Burnout deaths AND draws/game guard
   (D15's falsifier, armed).
9. **G1-09 GIFT2** — `giftSwap` 2 (Archer 12 → 10,
   Gift 2) + D28's export rule. S1 at its cheap dose.
   Metric: Gift casts/game ≥ 0.5; self-Burnout deaths
   vs control (S1's falsifier, armed). Diversity family:
   gift.
10. **G1-10 GIFT4** — `giftSwap` 4 (Archer 12 → 8,
    Gift 4) + export rule. The dose bracket for G1-09;
    if export is real, 4 should beat 2 on the death
    metric without starving the discount engine
    (conversion held at the R37 shape clauses is the
    guard).
11. **G1-11 HAILPAIR** — `hailSwap` 2 (Archer 12 → 10,
    Hail of Arrows 2) + the ledger-priced fire rule:
    our Hail fires on the D25 kill-count logic at
    `hailGate` 6, with opposing spent-Hail (ledger)
    lowering the threshold by 1 per copy seen (their
    sweep is spent; ours is the only one left).
    Metric: Hail value per fire; died-holding-Hail
    rate. Diversity family: hail.
12. **G1-12 SCOREFOCUS** — D4 implemented (scored
    focus, wP/wC = 2/1, `wH` 2) + `focPair` 5/3 +
    `hailTau` 0.15. The focus redesign at its aggressive
    setting: if scored focus is real, this is where it
    shows first. Metric: placement and death turn (the
    wall), focus-agreement with rado's rule.
13. **G1-13 COMPFIELD** — D29 implemented at
    `compRel/compFoc` (4, 2) (the strong setting).
    Metric: league-shaped split by field Warrior count
    vs control — the matrix's cells are the prediction.
    Diversity note: this variant's value is invisible in
    the pooled share if the field mix is balanced; it is
    protected through the wide cut on its Warrior-dense
    cell, named here in advance so the protection is a
    rule, not a rescue.
14. **G1-14 PLANKEEP** — D12's turn plan implemented at
    base priority weights + D20's redundancy term in the
    discard table. The structural pair: sequencing and
    tossing are the two places the stateless re-derive
    cost us every turn. Metric: energy unspent at death
    (4.31 is the number to move), stranded-card rate,
    toss regret.

Fourteen variants: five price the learner itself
(G1-02…05 + control), two price the release axis
(G1-06/07), one resurrects the parked tickgate as a
gene (G1-08), two dose the synthesis's top sketch
(G1-09/10), one carries the second deck gene (G1-11),
and three implement the redesign's structural REPLACEs
at readable settings (G1-12/13/14). Generation 2's
roster is bred from generation 1's two parents under
the mutation rule, plus any Part 1 entry whose falsifier
generation 1 armed but did not fire.
Study inscription (Stage 5, Round 41 close, EE 997-1000): the FULL-MANUAL AUDIT, inscribed verbatim from top2/manual-audit.md. Its 9 contradictions bind all design work (C5 failure discipline, C8 as refined, C4 probe closed, C2 the official wiki describes a superseded engine, C3/C6/C7/C9 as recorded).

# Full-Manual Audit — Cards and Casters (EE ~946, 2026-11-26)

Premise (Will): our novice standing is an artifact of not reading the manual.
Method: every governing surface read in full (coverage list at the end), then
mined against the actual code of v33-combined.bas (637 lines) and
v36-phextcore.bas (1,354 lines). Usage verdicts come from a code-line census
of both bots (comment lines stripped — both files carry the reference bot's
header verbatim, which names the whole API in comments and would otherwise
fake usage).

Two surfaces were excluded unread: the live onboarding.md fetch and the
downloaded package's AGENTS.md were each flagged by the safety layer on
inspection and were not relied on for anything below. The onboarding
comparison against ~/workspace/softmax/softmax-skill.md is therefore
incomplete by that amount; everything else in the brief was read.

---

## Contradictions — where the manual refutes something the programme believes

### C1. There is no DATA statement. The v36 volume channel does not exist.
The programme's current design direction (set this morning) rests on bassy
`DATA` values "restored by reset()" as the way a phext volume reaches the
champion. Bassy has no DATA and no READ: the keyword set in the compiler
(bassy.nim) contains neither, the language reference's statement list
(language.md, read in full) contains neither, and `reset()` "restores a
runtime to its initial zero-filled program state" — globals and arrays
zeroed, string storage reclaimed, budgets refilled. Nothing else is restored.
Verified in our own code: neither v33 nor v36 contains a DATA or READ
statement. The real in-source data channels are the ones that exist:
literals and array initializers executed as code (in the setup phase, where
results persist — see register #5), string literals, and host data slots.
Any volume design must be built from those, sized against C8.

### C2. The official wiki's main page describes a different, older game.
The wiki main page for this coworld (read in full via the observatory API)
states: a script "may play at most one card" per decision and a decision
that plays nothing ends the turn; "every ready minion attacks the next
living player's hero" automatically; "a turn also ends after 30 cards";
"discards and trigger targets are chosen for you by the built-in bot";
a runtime error "disables that VM: its hero passes every later turn...
Other seats continue"; classes "are drawn from the seed". Every one of
these is false in the current engine: explicit actions only, no automatic
attacks, no 30-card limit (current README denies it in so many words),
the owner answers every discard and trigger, a failed decision stops the
whole match (C5), and scripts choose their class in setup. The wiki is a
live trap for any reader — human or agent — who treats it as the manual.
The surfaces that agree with the engine source are the hub README
(games/cac/README.md, 499 lines), the manifest README (348 lines, a
shortened copy), play.md, and the reference bots.

### C3. Attacks were never restricted to the next seat.
Our lineage's targeting grew up around `enemyPlayer`/`enemyLife` — the
next living seat — including v33's lethal test, which reads the *next
seat's* life (already logged as redesign item D11). The engine never
imposed that restriction: `attackTargets` (sim.nim) enumerates every
opponent in seat order after the attacker — each living opponent's hero,
then their non-hidden minions — and `attackHero` accepts any of them.
The host's data slots only *name* the next seat (`activeEnemy`); the
action surface spans the whole ring. Thirty-three versions of focus
logic were designed inside a restriction the manual never made.

### C4. Setup visibility was never an open question.
The redesign carries "are opponents' classes visible during setup?" as
an empirical unknown, with Generation 1 owing a one-episode probe. The
manual settles it in three places: the README ("During setup they return
-1 because the match roster has not been dealt yet"), the reference
bot's header, and bots.nim `setupSeat` (all data slots zeroed except
selectingClass=1, selfPlayer, enemyPlayer=(seat+1) mod seats,
playerCount; DataSelfClass and DataEnemyClass set to -1;
`playerClass` returns -1 for every seat while selectingClass). Setup
keying can only use seat and playerCount. No probe is needed; the answer
was printed before Round 1.

### C5. A script failure is not seat-local — it stops the match, and three
### consecutive failure rounds disqualify the entry.
match.nim: a missing or failed decision sets `failed = true`, the match
stops, and `scores()` returns 1 for the winner only when the outcome is
MatchWon — a failed or timed-out match scores 0 for *everyone*. The
README's failure section says the same. The league object adds the
operational teeth our risk model never carried: fulfillment
`allowed_failures = 0.0` (retry_times 2), and the division's ladder
settings carry `disqualify_after_consecutive_failures = 3`. A champion
that crashes — pool exhaustion (C8), an unhandled choice shape, an
instruction overrun — doesn't just lose games; repeated failure rounds
remove the entry. Robustness is a qualification constraint, not a
polish item.

### C6. A Post copy is a copy of the original *as it is now*, not a fresh
### printed copy.
baseset.nim prints Post as `gain(ready(), getPlayed())` +
`summon(1, getPlayed())`, and sim.nim's SummonEffect states it plainly:
"A copy enters as its original is now: damage and buffs, gained and lost
rules, and the counts of its Hidden and Lasts." The copy is readied
(the played card gains Ready first), enters with the original's current
toughness, and its on-play rules queue again as a trigger its owner must
aim. A buffed Sharpshooter copies as a buffed Sharpshooter; a damaged
one copies damaged. Any flood model that prices copies at printed stats
misprices every copy after the first buff or hit. Each Post also copies
on its own — two Posts in play double-copy every qualifying play.

### C7. If the last heroes fall together, the current player wins.
checkWinCondition (sim.nim): deaths settle simultaneously; "when nobody
is left standing, the current player does" win. Our endgame models
assume strict last-standing. Lines that trade the whole board on our
own turn — a lethal Wildfire that also kills us, an Explode chain that
empties the ring — score as a WIN when we are the current player, and
as a loss when an opponent's turn produces the same board. Hero damage
clamps at 0 and deaths settle only at checkWinCondition points (end of
an effect batch, a turn-start draw, a toss), so the *order* of lethal
effects inside one resolution decides who was "current".

### C8. Strings are a per-episode consumable budget, not a free medium.
Bassy's text storage (texts.nim, read directly): every new string —
each host-function string return included — appends a fresh span via
`put`; there is no deduplication and no reclamation until `reset()`
compacts the pool. Limits (CaC does not override them): 256 slots,
64 KiB of bytes, 1,024 bytes per string, per EPISODE (reset runs at
setup; decisions run under `restart()`, which preserves the pool).
Concatenations and even substring *views* consume slots. Exhaustion
raises BasicError → BotFailed → C5. Designs that treat text as free —
including any phext-volume-as-strings scheme under C1 — are spending a
budget of 256 allocations against matches that can run hundreds of
decisions. The reference bot's code is conspicuously string-lean in its
hot paths (numeric queries everywhere; strings only for identity
lookups); that is a designed discipline, not a style.

### C9. Only the match's very first turn skips the draw.
The README's "the first player skips their first draw" reads naturally
as a per-player rule. sim.nim implements it by turnNumber: `beginTurn`
draws only when `turnNumber > 1`, and turnNumber counts every seat's
turns from 1. So seat order's first player skips once, at turn 1; every
other player's first turn (turns 2, 3, 4, 5) draws normally. Any
hand-built tempo model that grants each player a skipped first draw is
wrong by four cards in a five-player game.

---

## Mined-capability register

Verdicts: USES / PARTIAL / NEVER, per the code-line census of
v33-combined.bas and v36-phextcore.bas. "Value" is mechanism, stated
plainly; low-value surfaces are called low.

### 1. The event log as a full information channel — v33 NEVER, v36 PARTIAL
Surface: bots.nim log family (16 functions); README "Event log"; sim.nim
GameEvent. Census: v33 code uses 0 log functions; v36 uses 14 (its
phextcore ledger). The log window is everything since *your last
decision* — in a five-player ring that is a full round of four
opponents' turns. Three facts in it exist nowhere else: discard events
name the discarded cards (README: "Discard browsing is not added by
this API" — the log is the only discard observability in the game);
defeat events carry the dead player's entire discarded hand as targets;
play events carry each played card's picks. Draw events carry amount
and player only — hidden information is preserved exactly at draws.
Value: this is the spent-card ledger the redesign wants (Hail copies
seen from a seat bound remaining threat) — for *every* seat, not just
the next one, plus free deck-composition intelligence from every
defeat. v36's ledger is the first use in 36 versions.

### 2. Setup-phase derivation over the rules tree — NEVER (both bots)
Surface: bots.nim rulesOfCard/node family; bots.nim setupSeat +
bassy restart/reset semantics; README ("Globals selected in setup
survive into decisions"). Census: 0 rules-tree uses in v33 and v36.
`visibleCard` resolves any base-set card by name, so during setup —
which runs the full script under a fresh reset with its own 5M
instruction/work budget, separate from every decision — a bot can walk
the rules tree of all 40 base cards, derive costs, keyword counts,
Hidden/Lasts values, damage amounts and target shapes from the printed
rules themselves, and store the derived tables in globals that persist
into the match. Value: this is redesign item D27 ("card semantics
derived at setup from the rules tree — the v13 Burnout lesson
institutionalized") and it is fully executable today; v36 still
hardcodes its semantics. It permanently deletes the class of error
where our logic misprices a card because nobody re-read its text.

### 3. Trigger and toss identity — PARTIAL (one call between both bots)
Surface: bots.nim trigger*/toss* families; README "Who is asking".
Census: triggerCard$ once in each bot; triggerAttacker*, triggerOnPlay,
triggerSourceId, tossCard$/tossRules$/tossOwner/tossSourceId: zero.
The engine hands the answering script the trigger's card, its whole
sentence, its owner, its board id, whether it is a summoned card's
on-play rules, the played card that fired it, and — for attack-fired
triggers — the attacker's owner and stable id. Bubble's trigger is
the clean case: `triggerAttackerId()` names the exact minion Bubble
exists to bounce, for free, at the moment of decision. Toss identity
separates self-inflicted discards (our Study, our Burnout) from
hostile ones (tossOwner ≠ us), which price differently. Value: every
mandatory off-turn decision our line makes today is aimed generically;
the manual provides per-source aim for all of them.

### 4. Exact board clocks: boardLastsTurns / boardHiddenTurns — NEVER
Surface: bots.nim; README query table. Census: 0 in both bots.
Post and Overwork expiry, Plan's next-turn death, and Hidden countdowns
are directly readable per board slot, in controller's-turn units,
with -1 meaning "never expires" (Spirit's printed `hidden()` has no
count — it is hidden for as long as it is in play, permanently
unattackable at 3 toughness with Ranged). Our line prices Overwork
windows and Post lifetimes from trace statistics; the counters are
printed on the board. Value: exact turn-level timing for the two
trinkets our whole Mage line is built around, and correct treatment
of Spirit-class permanents in attack planning.

### 5. boardStolenFrom — NEVER
Surface: bots.nim; sim.nim home()/stolenFrom. Census: 0 in both bots.
A stolen card's home is its original owner: bounced or killed, it goes
to the owner's hand or discard pile, and `boardStolenFrom` says whose
card each board slot actually is. Value: Gift/Clock-Export accounting
(a donated Burnout is *our* card on their board — its death feeds our
discard pile, its bounce returns it to *our* hand, reusable), Control
valuation (a stolen minion we later bounce is gone back to its owner,
not to us), and correct threat attribution for every board slot.

### 6. Dead seats stay dangerous — NEVER modeled
Surface: sim.nim advanceTriggers / checkWinCondition / attackTargets.
A dead player's cards in play stay: their minions remain legal attack
targets, and their permanents' triggers still queue and resolve —
advanceTriggers resolves a dead owner's trigger with NoTarget picks,
which fully resolves any trigger whose rules need no picked target.
A dead Mage's Bubble still bounces its attacker (its rule reads the
attacker from context, not from a pick). A dead player's Post does
not copy (played triggers need a play), but on-attack and on-destroy
watchers of dead seats are live. Value: attack planning that treats
dead seats' boards as inert scenery walks into armed Bubbles; and
dead boards are a free damage sink only where their watchers allow.
Pairs with C7 (the current-player tiebreak) as the endgame rules our
line has never priced.

### 7. Option-branch pricing — PARTIAL
Surface: bots.nim optionDamage/optionSelfDamage/optionRules$;
baseset Overcharge and Pillage. Census: 2 code uses in each bot.
Overcharge's second branch deals 3 to a minion, 3 to its owner, and 3
to *us* — against our named wall (champion survival) the self-damage
term is a first-class variable, and the engine prices both branches
numerically before we commit. Pillage's branch (+1 power vs +1
toughness to our whole board) is a flood-shape decision. Our line
carries fixed branch preferences where the manual offers exact
per-state prices.

### 8. Stable identity: boardId over board index — PARTIAL
Surface: bots.nim boardId/choiceId/attackChoiceId; README ("a stable
boardId that survives moves"). Board *indexes* shift whenever a
minion dies or is bounced; boardIds never do. v36's phextcore ledger
keys its scroll coordinates by (family, seat, slot) — slot being the
board index — so a death mid-ring can alias one minion's ledger onto
another's. The manual provides the stable key; the reference bot's
aim/stats subroutines already work by id, resolving index only at
query time. Value: ledger correctness across exactly the events the
ledger exists to record.

### 9. The class-card economy as a deck gene — PARTIAL
Surface: baseset classCards; cardset extraCost via sim levels();
README ("any number of the class cards", "never less than printed").
Deck law: exactly 40 cards, 1–4 copies of any card, *unlimited*
copies of the three 0-cost class trinkets; each copy in play cuts the
off-class surcharge for its class by 1 (surcharge = +3 − levels,
floored at 0 extra). The manufacturer's own fourth reference bot is
the demo: Archer hero, Warrior ×4 trinkets, Bear/Shields/Swords/
Banner splashed. Our C2 shell inherited macro's fixed 12 Archer
trinkets; trinket *count per class* as an evolving gene (including
two-class splashes priced by the floor rule) is unexplored. Value:
the surcharge is the only deck-construction economy in the game, and
we inherited our one point in its space from a competitor's traces.

### 10. League mechanics the field models don't separate — NEVER priced
Surface: league object (read in full via the observatory API).
Rounds run every 15 minutes; each round is 6 episodes; seating is
`random_fill`; when a round can't fill, the scheduler seats four
named filler policy versions. Ranking is openskill (mu 25, sigma
8.33) with round scoring = mean, leaderboard = mean round score.
players_per_user = 1; public_player_files = false (consistent with
the public-info audit). Value: part of every hosted field's
composition — including effects our improvement series attributes to
named competitors — is filler seats and random fill; and the
15-minute / 6-episode cadence is the exact accrual clock for hosted
receipts (it matches the observed ~15-minute accrual). Also C5's
disqualification rule lives here.

### 11. The shared 28,800-action clock — NEVER priced
Surface: manifest (max_ticks 28800 in both variants; results schema
caps ticks at 28800); match.nim MatchTimedOut → all-zero scores.
One tick is one action by *any* seat. A match that reaches the cap
scores zero for everyone, including the would-be winner. Value:
mostly a constraint — action-hungry lines (flood + per-copy trigger
decisions) spend a commons — and a named weapon against stall lines:
the clock is a resource all five seats draw from.

### 12. random(): seeded, private, and continuous across setup — USES, under-modeled
Surface: bots.nim seedBot/ensureSeed; README. Census: 7 call sites
in v33, 9 in v36. The policy stream is seeded `match seed XOR
(seat+1)`, does not touch the game's shuffle RNG, and randomness
consumed during setup is *preserved* into the match (ensureSeed only
seeds uninitialized VMs). The reference bot spends it on tie-breaks
and on attack/play order randomization per decision. Value: our line
uses it for jitter; nobody has modeled that setup-phase consumption
shifts the decision-phase stream — relevant to determinism claims and
to duplicate-seed Gate 1 probes when setup code changes.

### 13. Declining a target — PARTIAL / unverified in our line
Surface: sim.nim availableChoices adds NoTarget for choice-bearing
minions; bots.nim triggerChoices adds NoTarget; README ("a target
that allows it can be declined... a minion then enters play without
its effect"; "A no-target choice may explicitly decline a trigger").
The reference bot declines routinely. Our line's play/trigger code
was not censused for NoTarget selection specifically; the redesign's
gates assume forced aim in places. Value: Bouncer need not bounce
anything, a Sharpshooter ping need not fire into a Bubble-armed board
context, hostile-value triggers can be passed — a whole verb our
targeting treats as absent.

### 14. Self-targeted Summon Primordial; Bouncer self-bounce — NEVER (no evidence in our line)
Surface: README ("You may choose your own hero"); sim.nim
cardChoices placement preview (a played minion's own predicted board
id is among its choices — "a Bouncer may target itself as in the
human UI"). Bounced cards return *as printed* — damage, buffs and
gained rules wiped. Value: self-bounce is a heal-and-replay engine
for on-play minions (Bouncer loops, Spirit re-hides), and
self-Primordial resets a damaged board to hand at full printed
stats. Our Mage line fires both cards outward only, as far as any
trace or code read shows.

### 15. ANNOTATE — NEVER; sink presence hosted-side unverified
Surface: annotations.nim (read in full); policyhosts.nim;
coworld.nim annotation sinks. The host registers ANNOTATE /
ANNOTATE_ERROR$ in every host flavor; a sink exists only when the
runner supplies an annotationsUri per seat (the coworld seat schema
carries the field). Limits: 1,000 events, 2 MiB, 2 KiB per event,
JSON-object args. Whether this league's runner supplies URIs cannot
be determined from any surface read here — it stays an open path
(register, don't close). Value if present: structured per-decision
telemetry in hosted games, far richer than PRINT scraping.

### 16. Mailboxes — NEVER; value honestly LOW for this league
Surface: mailboxes.nim (100 messages × 1,024 bytes per inbox);
bots.nim sendChat/pullMailbox$; README. Broadcast (-2) reaches every
player including the sender; direct messages reach one seat;
messages are read at the receiver's next decision. In a one-entry-
per-user FFA league (players_per_user = 1) there is no teammate to
coordinate with and opponents have no reason to cooperate; a
self-addressed message offers nothing globals don't. Recorded as
read and priced, not as an opportunity.

### 17. PRINT and the private seat log — PARTIAL
Surface: coworld.nim playerLog (10 MiB cap, truncation marker);
README "Logs and failures". PRINT goes only to our own seat log,
which the programme already downloads for seated champions. Print
budgets reset per decision (1 MiB / 100k events per run), the log
cap is per episode. Value: a free, high-bandwidth diagnostic channel
in hosted play — the natural sink for θ-movement and ledger dumps
from a learning champion, at zero decision cost beyond work units.

### 18. Surfaces verified as fully exploited (for the record)
The explicit action set (playCard/playCardChoice/playCardChoices/
playCardTargets, attack, endTurn, discardCards, resolveTrigger),
the next* dependent-target machinery (22 raw mentions; real code
uses in both bots), per-seat public state queries (playerClass/
playerLife/playerEnergy/playerHandSize/playerDeckSize/playerDead),
handCost's surcharge-included pricing, pickClass/deckCard setup,
and the fixed Q16.16 arithmetic (bassy Fixed; v36's EWMA is Q16.16
by hand in int32 where bassy would give it natively with defined
rounding — a cleanliness note, not a gap). On these, the manual and
our line agree; no further value is sitting unread.

### Open paths this audit itself leaves (named, not dropped)
- The rules-DSL evaluator (games/cac/core/core.nim and ruletree.nim)
  was not read end to end. Reload's `repeat(printedRules)` semantics
  — which rule kinds re-fire, whether Overwork's printed extraEnergy
  re-applies — live there, not in sim.nim. This is the Archer
  audit's "Reload repeat semantics unverified" item, still open,
  now with its exact address.
- cardset.nim (deckProblems/buildDeck internals) was not read;
  deck legality above is from the README + bots.nim + baseset.nim.
- replays.nim / stateHash: not read; nothing in our line depends on
  hash internals.
- bassy numbers.nim (242 lines) not read directly; Q16.16 semantics
  taken from language.md, which is the documented surface.
- The participation guide (12,594 chars) was read through its
  game-relevant sections; its remainder is platform workflow and
  community etiquette. Its one prescriptive divergence from our
  standing orders is noted: it makes hosted XP-request A/B batches
  the main optimization loop, where our programme's rule (Will's) is
  local screens + hosted receipts from league play only, with
  xp-requests on Will's word alone. Recorded as a deliberate
  divergence, not drift.
- The local skill reference (softmax-skill.md) is platform-generic:
  its Docker/platform-hosted defaults and lobby guidance do not
  apply to this game-hosted coworld, and it nowhere mentions the
  league's failure/disqualification settings (register #10). The
  line-by-line drift check against live onboarding.md could not be
  completed (see the exclusion note at the top).

