### 3c. Shared policy host (initPolicyHost, all CaC hosts)
ANNOTATE(4) cost 1024; ANNOTATE_ERROR$(0) cost 1. Never faults
(§1a).

### 3d. Compiler builtins (no host registration; TextCallOp)
From bassy/texts.nim `textFunction`: LEN, LEFT$, RIGHT$, MID$,
UCASE$, LCASE$, TRIM$, LTRIM$, RTRIM$, CHR$, ASC, SPACE$,
STRING$, INSTR, STR$ (15). Cost model (bassy.nim textCall):
chargeWork(1 + sum of argument string lengths); INSTR adds
len(haystack) x len(needle); SPACE$/STRING$ add the requested
length and fault above maxStringLength.

### 3e. Language grammar (reserved words, bassy.nim isReserved)
and, call, case, dim, do, else, elseif, end, eqv, exit, false,
for, gosub, goto, if, imp, is, let, loop, mod, next, not, on,
or, print, rem, return, select, step, stop, sub, then, to,
true, until, wend, while, xor, type, as — plus operators
+ - * / \ (integer divide) MOD, comparisons, AND/OR/XOR/EQV/IMP/
NOT, line numbers/labels, string concatenation by + (charged
per byte, bassy.nim addValue).

### 3f. Metering model — what 5M / 5M actually buys
- Both budgets refill at every restart() (i.e., per decision) and
  at reset(). botLimits sets both to 5,000,000.
- Plain code: the compiler inserts a MeterOp per basic block.
  Instructions are charged 1 per executed op (2 for ArrayGet /
  ArraySet / BufferGet / BufferSet). Work is charged per op by
  weight (workCost): 1 for most ops, 2 for immediate/global
  moves, 3-9 for the fused global ops, 4 for divide / modulo /
  print ops, 8 for CALL/GOSUB and ArrayAddGlobals.
- Host calls: work charged at the function's registered
  workUnits (validated at program load: HostCallOp cost must
  equal program.hostFunctions[id].workUnits); 1 instruction.
  Callbacks may charge more dynamically (chargeWork inside the
  callback — e.g., string returns allocate spans; string +
  concatenation charges per byte).
- String literal first materialization charges its length in
  work (LoadStringOp) and allocates a pool span (§4).
- At the expensive end, per decision: ~5,000,000 plain ops;
  50,000 action calls at 100 (the game ends long before);
  ~4,882 ANNOTATE calls at 1024; ~19,531 chat/mailbox calls at
  256; 250,000 card-text queries at 20; ~4 worst-case INSTR
  searches over two 1,024-char strings (1 + 1024 + 1024 +
  1024x1024 ≈ 1.05M work each). Exhaustion raises BasicError
  ("BASIC work limit exceeded" / "BASIC instruction limit
  exceeded") — a match-stopping fault under C5.

## 4. Storage and runtime state

### 4a. Numbers
int32 integers and Q16.16 Fixed (bassy/numbers.nim). Fixed
division rejects a zero divisor with BasicError ("division by
zero"); integer `div` is defined toward zero with defined
overflow. No floating point, no math library (no ABS/SIN/SQR
builtins exist — the only builtins are the 15 text functions).

### 4b. Strings (bassy/texts.nim — the pool)
Limits (bassy defaults, inherited by CaC): maxStrings = 256
spans, maxStringBytes = 64 KiB arena, maxStringLength = 1,024
chars per string. Every distinct string value created allocates
a span — there is no deduplication. Failures ("string length
limit exceeded" / "string count limit exceeded" / "string byte
limit exceeded") are BasicError. Lifecycle, verified in
bassy.nim restart() + texts.nim reset(): at each restart the
pool is compacted around its ROOTS — string globals, string
array cells, string host-data values, and materialized literals
— and everything else is reclaimed. So the per-episode
consumable is the rooted set; transient strings are per-decision
(the baseline build's C8 refinement, confirmed at source).
Literals materialize lazily on first evaluation and then root
for the rest of the episode — a program with N distinct string
literals pays N rooted spans once each is first touched.

### 4c. Arrays, globals, records
- Arrays: DIM with literal constant bounds, one-dimensional,
  top-level only; maxArrays = 256 arrays. Cells live in the VM
  memory array and persist across decisions; reset() restores
  each array's compile-time initial values — this array-initial
  mechanism is the entirety of "DATA" in bassy (reset()'s
  docstring says "the program's immutable DATA values"; there is
  no DATA statement — manual-audit C1, re-verified here).
- Globals: maxGlobals = 4,096 (numeric, string, and record
  homes); persist across decisions; zeroed at reset().
- Records: TYPE definitions with Integer/Fixed/Numeric/String
  fields, stored as ordinary global/memory cells. Unused by
  our line (§1e).
- Structural caps (defaults): maxRoutines 1,024, maxParameters
  64, maxCallDepth 64, maxRegisters 4,096, maxHostData 1,024,
  maxHostFunctions 256, maxCodeInstructions 1,000,000 compiled
  instructions (compile-time cap, distinct from the 5M runtime
  budget), maxSourceBytes 256 KiB (CaC override).
- Total VM memory is accounted at init against maxMemoryBytes =
  64 MiB ("BASIC runtime exceeds the configured memory limit"
  fails the load, before any decision).

### 4d. Buffers / blobs (bassy/buffers.nim) — present in the VM, unreachable in CaC
BufferStorage allows maxNativeBuffers = 256 buffers within
maxNativeMemoryBytes = 64 MiB (shared with §4c accounting);
each buffer costs 96 B bookkeeping plus payload. Buffers are
invalidated only at reset() — handles would persist across
decisions. But the only constructors/mutators (createBlob,
putBlob, blob binding) are host-side Runtime APIs, and the
script-facing registration (addBufferFunctions) is wired only
into the GotA neural host (§1f). In CaC this whole storage
class is inert.

### 4e. Output budgets
- PRINT: 1 MiB and 100,000 events PER DECISION (restart zeroes
  both counters — §1b), written to the seat's own log.
- ANNOTATE: 2 KiB per event, 1,000 events, 2 MiB per seat per
  sink (match lifetime), status-returning, never a fault (§1a).
- Mailboxes: recreated per match (resetInboxes); pullMailbox$
  on an empty inbox returns "" — never a fault. sendChat's
  target is a seat index (args[0]) with the message as args[1];
  broadcast/direct semantics live in mailboxes.nim's sendChat.

### 4f. Randomness and time
- `random(n)` is the ONLY entropy a script has. The stream is a
  std/random Rand seeded ONCE per episode at setup:
  setupSeat does runtime.reset() then seedBot(seed) with the
  match seed (bots.nim:1556); seedBot sets
  rng = initRand(seed xor (seat + 1)) (bots.nim:303).
  ensureSeed() preserves randomness consumed during setup — the
  stream is never reseeded per decision. runDecision falls back
  to seedBot(0) only if somehow unseeded. Policy randomness is
  separate from simulation randomness (seedBot's docstring).
  Deterministic per (episode seed, seat).
- No clock, no tick counter, no wall time is exposed. The only
  time-like observables are the turnNumber slot and logTurn.

### 4g. Error behavior (what faults, and what a fault costs)
- Compile time: an unresolvable name or type error fails the
  compile; loadBots keeps the seat with failed = true and the
  error message; the hosted path rejects the player
  (compilePlayer → rejectPlayer).
- Runtime: BasicError from — string pool exhaustion (§4b),
  checked array bounds, division by zero, work/instruction
  exhaustion, MID$ with start < 1, invalid/stale buffer handle
  (unreachable in CaC), native storage limits. A runtime
  BasicError surfaces as BotFailed in runDecision; under the
  league's failure rules (manual-audit C5) a failed decision
  stops the match and zeroes everyone, allowed_failures = 0.0,
  disqualification at 3 consecutive failures.
- Never faults: ANNOTATE (status return), pullMailbox$ on empty
  (""), log accessors on missing events (sentinels: -1 / 0 / ""),
  random(0) or negative (returns 0 — the proc guards args[0] > 0).

## 5. Usage contrast (comments stripped; call-site counts)

| Surface | v33 | v36 | emise-baseline |
|---|---|---|---|
| Host functions used (of 133) | 42 | 51 | 52 |
| Data slots read (of 19) | 9 | 11 | 12 |
| Text builtins used (of 15) | 0 | 7 | 7 |
| DIM arrays | 2 | 4 | 5 |
| TYPE definitions | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| PRINT statements | 0 | 0 | 4 |
| ANNOTATE calls | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| Mailbox calls | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| Suspect surfaces (str*/blob*/tensor*) | 0 | 0 | 0 |

Slots read — v33: selfPlayer, enemyPlayer, selfLife, enemyLife,
handSize, playerCount, tossCount, triggerTargets,
selectingClass. v36 adds currentPlayer, turnNumber.
Baseline adds nothing further. Never read by any: energy,
totalEnergy, selfBoardSize, enemyBoardSize, selfDeckSize,
enemyDeckSize, selfClass, enemyClass.

Growth v33 → baseline is real but narrow: the event log's core
accessors and boardStolenFrom were the only host families
adopted across 36 versions. The card database, the rules tree,
the trigger/toss identity surfaces, per-seat queries, mailboxes,
ANNOTATE, records, and 8 text builtins remain exactly as unused
as they were at v1 — and the three suspect surfaces the census
was ordered to check (tensors, neural blobs, the str* toolkit)
are not dormant capabilities but absent ones: registered only
in another game's host, or nowhere at all.
