### Class composition over time
- **Era 1 (Nov 7–13, 2,904 eps): the control-Mage era.** Deckwarden
  cluster + greenshank + stilt-Mage + rado-Mage hold most seats; death-turn
  medians 54–58; A-aron v142 (0.226) is the only flood Mage at strength.
  Fillers and the AWM Baseline seat here and never again.
- **Era 2 (Nov 14–20, 4,020 eps): the freeze forms.** rado v166, macro v64,
  stilt v30 (Warrior), salvo seat from Nov 14; daf's empty seat enters
  rotation (1,151 g). Shares: rado .405, macro .285, A-aron .205.
- **Era 3 (Nov 21–26, 3,318 eps): the current field.** Eight actives at
  ~12% seatshare each; daf in 59% of episodes; Rohit's orch-7 (0.207)
  until cac-rado replaces it Nov 24; we seat Nov 24 (v16) and Nov 26
  (v30); Eckstar enters Nov 26. Death medians compress to 25–29 — the
  field got faster as it froze.

### Entry / exit / freeze timeline
- Oct 1: league created; AWM Baseline seated at creation minute.
- Oct 2–17: richard/relh deckwarden project (identical source, two
  accounts; Oct 17 restoration after a champion-loss event, W-5).
- Nov 10–14: the great versioning burst — macro's 64 in 3.8 days, rado's
  run to v166, A-aron's jumps to v142, stilt to v30. **Nov 14: the top
  five freeze.** Since then, league-wide submissions: Rohit (Nov 17
  orch-7 v2; Nov 24 cac-rado), docxology's verbatim re-seat (Nov 14),
  Eckstar (Nov 26), and ours (Nov 24/26). Nothing from the top five in
  12 days.
- Nov 24: we enter (v16), Rohit switches orch-7 → cac-rado.
- Nov 26: v30 succeeds v16; Eckstar joins as the 10th member.

### Rating mechanics — MMR moves without builds moving
- Andre's mean has been pinned at 0.343–0.347 from EE 588 to EE 932 while
  his MMR swung 24.71 → 27.76 → 25.73 → 26.37 (±1.5) on an **unchanged**
  v166. He took rank #1 at EE 846 with no mean change at all — macro's
  MMR decayed 26.94 → 25.61 over the same span on an unchanged v64.
  **MMR here is a redistribution dial, not a form meter**: at flat means,
  rating flows toward whoever the *field's composition* currently favors
  (our entry, Eckstar's entry, Rohit's switch, daf's rotation share all
  repriced the incumbents).
- Per-publish improvement is **unmeasured for every competitor** — no
  dated observation brackets a version change, because all versioning
  happened inside the Nov 10–14 burst between sparse early pulls. The
  version-era shares in the index (rado v91 .285 → v166 .394; stilt Mage
  v21 .351 → v24 .050 → Warrior v30 .199; greenshank v30 .306 → v33
  .053) are the honest substitute: version changes moved shares by
  ±10–25 points inside a week, then froze.
- Eckstar's MMR is decaying on arrival (12.27 → 11.99, mean sliding
  .111 → .090 as n grows): the rating system prices a new weak seat
  downward as evidence accumulates — the mirror image of Andre's drift.

### The dead seat, priced field-wide (share with daf seated − without)
macro **+11.0 pts**, salvo **+10.0**, emise-v16 +8.6, emise-v30 +5.5,
A-aron +4.5, cac-rado +3.5, rado +2.7, stilt +1.7, eckstar −0.6,
richard −1.6. The bye is worth most to the Post/Gift Mage family (one
fewer live clock racing the flood) and nothing to Warriors. Any
evaluation of a Mage candidate that ignores whether daf was in the room
misprices it by up to ±10 points of composition.

### Timing
Episode volume is flat around the clock (408–432 episodes/hour UTC, all
24 hours; ~86 rounds/day). There is **no time-of-day composition effect**
— seating is uniform random_fill over members at all hours. Round cadence
matches the manual audit (15-min rounds × 6 episodes).

## 6. Seat logs and table talk — what the seats actually say

- **Player logs are ownership-gated** (correction, see head). Readable set
  = our own seats only: **468 log files (v6/v16/v30 episodes), 5 distinct
  hashes total — the seat variants of the standard text**
  "Player slot N started.\n\nPlayer slot N completed.\n" (49 bytes).
  **We PRINT nothing; no seat we can read prints anything.** Our own bots
  have never used PRINT, sendChat, or ANNOTATE in hosted play.
- **Game logs (4,336 downloaded)** are one infrastructure template
  (container headers, sidecar startup, winner line); 18 of the child's
  sampled 436 end "stopped: maximum ticks" (zero-score timeouts). No
  gameplay text, no chat records.
- **Chat/mailbox:** no readable artifact — game log, player log, or
  replay event stream — contains any chat content from any seat. Whether
  rivals exchange mailbox traffic is **unobservable from outside their
  seats**; the honest statement is that the channel shows zero evidence
  of use, and the only way to learn if the field talks is to talk and
  watch for replies in our own seat's mailbox.
- Talk-persona implication (§8b): the personas cannot be calibrated on
  observed field talk, because there is none on record. They must be
  justified as *probes*, not as models of observed behavior.

## 7. Intent vs behavior — the submissions corpus, verified

Corpus: 268 entries, only 78 (29.1%) carry any text; all prose = 7,941
chars. The three biggest submitters (rado 101, Andrew 66, A-aron 7) wrote
**nothing**. Verified claims (trace-checked this study):

- **R-1 (Rohit, cac-rado recipe) — mostly true, two clauses overclaim.**
  "Hail only vs 3+ enemy minions": **196 of 197 casts comply** (99.5%).
  "Rally/Commander before Hail": 36 of 197 Hails (18%) precede any
  Rally/Commander — a tendency, not the stated ordering. "Bear first at
  2 energy": Bear is his first play in 170/616 games (27.6%); Footsoldier
  leads (320). The recipe is real for the Hail gate, loose elsewhere.
- **E-1 (Eckstar) — true, and it doesn't matter.** 220/220 Swords/Shields
  plays with a minion on board, 0 violations; deck is the untouched stock
  precon. E-3 (his disclosed 0/10 scrimmage) matches his hosted 0.093.
  The most honest prose in the corpus describes the weakest seat.
- **D-1/D-2 (docxology) — the prose describes a bot that never played.**
  The tie-break build's deck is empty in 3,123/3,123 traced seats; the
  "+5 predecessor tie-break" is unimplemented in the seated file, and the
  title's "v3" is the submitted v2. His preregistered-screen numbers
  (D-4) may be true of some local file; they are not true of the seat.
- **M-2/M-8 (macromackie)** — partially checkable: his five seated deck
  combos and Post disciplines match the diary's Warrior-board phase
  description in shape; the v31 "one rule" root and the v5/v8, v20/v30
  re-entry identities concern versions that seat too rarely in-window to
  verify behaviorally (named, not resolved). M-1's gate (3,000 games,
  0 failures) is consistent with C5 discipline but not independently
  verifiable from outside.
- **W-1 (richard/relh identical source)** — consistent with traces: both
  body-deck seats run one deck variant with near-identical profiles
  (richard 0.099 / relh 0.098 full-window); the Oct-window originals are
  outside the reachable harvest, so identity is supported, not proven.
- **F-1/F-2 (hail-warrior)** — v1/v2 seat outside the reachable window;
  the in-window v3 fields Control instead of the described package and
  casts no Hail at all — the line drifted from its prose before it
  benched. F-4 stands: salvo, the seated build, has no prose.
- **U-3 (us)** — our v30 was submitted with no text at all, the same
  absence pattern as every other seated build that matters (rado v166,
  macro v64 since v40, salvo v1). The field's norm at the top is silence;
  our v6/v16 prose is the exception, and it is public (§ mirror-implication
  already recorded: keep submission notes terse).

General law from the corpus: **prose quality and seat strength are
uncorrelated** — the most rigorous diary (docxology) seats an empty deck;
the most accurate recipe (Rohit) describes a mid-field Warrior; the two
strongest seats wrote nothing at all. Read prose for *what to verify*,
never for *what is true*.

## 8. What this changes

### (a) League-shaped table construction
1. **Seat the era-3 field, not the top-2 caricature.** Recipe per
   5-seat table, from era-3 seatshares: rado, macro, A-aron, salvo,
   stilt at member frequency; richard in ~70% of tables (he is in ~70%
   of episodes); **daf's empty seat in ~40–59% of tables** (priced at
   +5.5 pts for our Mage family — a candidate graded without daf
   rotation is graded on a harder field than the league's); cac-rado /
   orch-7 as the rotating Warrior; fillers **never** as a routine seat
   (<3% of episodes, and 0% in the current era).
2. **Replicas warranted, in order:** (i) **macro refresh** — the current
   replica understates the seat that decides our share; nothing else
   matters until this is fixed. (ii) **salvo** — v30's #2 killer, no
   replica. (iii) **A-aron** — the field's purest focus rule (atk_low
   .923, zero creature attacks) is cheap to replicate exactly and
   stresses a different axis than rado's scored rule. (iv) **richard** —
   a control-Mage stand-in to restore hosted game length (his rooms run
   to turn 31+; our tables' death median is ~3.5 turns too fast without
   him). daf needs no replica — an empty seat *is* the replica.
   stilt/orch-7: orch-7's traced profile suffices as the stock-Warrior
   filler; no separate stilt replica warranted.
3. **Report shares split by daf presence and by macro presence** as
   standing practice — the two composition terms (±5–20 pts) are larger
   than most mechanism effects we screen for.

### (b) Table-talk loop personas
The measured field justifies **no talk persona from observation** — there
is no observed talk. What the field *does* justify:
- **The silent control is the field's actual behavior.** Every readable
  seat is silent; the control arm isn't a baseline fiction, it's the
  league's norm. Any talk effect measured locally is an effect *against
  silence*, and the burden of proof stays on talk.
- **Personas as probes, ranked by what they'd teach us:** a *trusting*
  persona tests whether coordination offers get honored by *our own*
  future hosted opponents only when a talk-carrying seat is hosted —
  locally it can only measure our bot's robustness. The locally
  meaningful personas are the **adversarial** ones (deceptive, skeptical)
  used as *defensive* tests: does our talk module leak information, get
  manipulated, or waste its 256-work messages? The field data adds one
  concrete design input: the seats that would benefit most from
  coordination against the leader are the mid-field Mages (salvo,
  A-aron — both Gift donors with distributed/pure-focus targeting);
  if talk ever matters hosted, it matters there first.
- One asymmetry the logs *do* prove: **nobody PRINTs telemetry either.**
  Our proposed telemetry hunk (bot PRINTs its thetas at death) would
  make us the only seat in the league putting state in a public log —
  readable by us, and (per the ownership gate) *only* by us. The gate
  cuts our way here: our seat log is private to us in practice.

### (c) Generation 2 design
1. **Design against macro, not against the field average.** The gap to
   the bar is one seat's conversion rate. Gen 2's mechanism questions
   should be posed in macro's terms: what survives a Post flood that
   converts at his rate, what kills a Mage whose board maxes at 10 while
   dying first only 17.7% of the time — with the refreshed replica as
   the sparring partner and hosted receipts as the judge.
2. **Class is a phase, not an identity — the champions proved it.** rado
   and Andrew both reached their peaks only after crossing classes;
   Andrew's *best* build was a Mage he abandoned after one collapse
   (v24, 0.050, n=239 — a real signal, but his Warrior line never
   matched the Mage's peak either). Gen 2's portfolio rule (D1/D2)
   should treat the Mage/Warrior choice as per-generation evidence, and
   the unbuilt Archer question stays open with better evidence than we
   had: volley's traced Archer (0.188, Post/Reload/Bolt) is a working
   existence proof inside the real field, and the Archer shell sketch
   now has a measured reference build to price against.
3. **Exploit the two structural gifts the field hands us:** the dead
   seat's +5.5–11 pts to Mage-family seats (already in our share — Gen 2
   should *protect* it by not being the seat that fills the room
   differently), and richard's uniformity (he taxes everyone ~equally
   and beats nobody; long games against him favor the build with the
   better late clock — our Burnout discipline is already banked).
4. **Speed is a field property now.** Era-3 death medians (25–29) vs
   era-1 (54–58): the frozen field kills twice as fast as the field our
   early lineage was tuned in. Survival mechanisms (Gen 1's purpose)
   should be judged at era-3 tempo, not corpus-average tempo.

