## Results — v39-lethalfix vs v38-gift2 (traced sample, paired)

| metric | v38-gift2 | v39-lethalfix |
|---|---|---|
| win share | 23.0% (69/300) | 21.0% (63/300) |
| paired wins | — | both 63, **v39-only 0, v38-only 6** |
| placement | 3.02 | 3.04 |
| death turn | 29.2 | 29.2 |
| **spared-executioner losses** | **95/231 = 41.1%** | **91/237 = 38.4%** |
| spared turns in losses | 136 | 131 |
| Gift casts | 140 (0.47/g) | 140 (0.47/g) |
| clocks donated / donated kills | 140 / 17 | 140 / 19 |
| self-Burnout deaths | 15 (6.5% of losses) | 16 (6.8% of losses) |

The export is unharmed (casts, donations identical; kills and
self-Burnout within noise). The wall is unmoved (death turn 29.2
both arms).

## Decomposition — where the spared turns actually live

Spared turns in losses, decomposed from the traces (this leg's
mining, both arms):

| class | v38 (of 136) | v39 (of 131) |
|---|---|---|
| sum-only lethal (no single minion's power ≥ hero life) | 108 (79%) | 122 (93%) |
| single-swing lethal available | 28 (21%) | **9 (7%)** |
| spared hero burning | 28 (21%) | 29 (22%) |
| W4 shape (burning + life ≤ 8 + sum-only) | 20 (15%) | 20 (15%) |
| spared hero is the next seat (old test read the right life) | 33 (24%) | 42 (32%) |
| spared hero died later anyway | 106 (78%) | 96 (73%) |

## Verdict

**The defect is real; the fix works on its own class; the D11
hypothesis is wrong about the binding path.**

- The fix does what it says: spared turns where a single swing
  could have killed fell **28 → 9 (−68%)**. As code, the per-target
  test is simply correct — the old line priced a game-ending swing
  by a bystander's life.
- But that class is only a fifth of the spared population. **79% of
  v38's spared turns are sum-only lethals**: the kill exists only
  as the SUM of several ready minions' power against one hero, and
  no per-swing test — fixed or not — can ever see it, because each
  swing is priced alone. The headline metric therefore barely
  moved (41.1% → 38.4% of losses, inside the ~4.5-point noise band
  at this n; turns 136 → 131), and the paired win read went the
  other way (v39-only 0 wins, v38-only 6): the old test's phantom
  100s (next seat low, target not lethal) were pushing face swings
  that turned out to be worth taking, and removing them cost more
  than the true conversions gained in this sample. The audit's own
  pre-stated falsifier — "missed-lethal count identical between
  arms at depth → the defect was unreachable in practice; record
  and revert" — is effectively met (131 vs 136).
- What the traces suggest instead: the binding path is
  **turn-level lethal arithmetic**. A mechanism that sums ready
  power per enemy hero at turn start and, when the sum covers a
  hero's life, sequences every swing at that hero (overriding
  per-swing trade values) addresses the 79% class directly. That
  is the redesign's D12 turn-plan territory, not a one-line test
  fix. Secondary named contributors: W4's deliberate burning-hero
  spares (15% of spared turns are exactly W4's shape — a banked
  mechanism betting the clock finishes him; in 78% of spared turns
  the hero did die later, but in ~22% of spared turns he went on
  to win the game) and the teamcalc partner-zero, which is the
  plausible residual explanation for most of v39's remaining 9
  single-swing spared turns (a granted 100 still loses to a
  partner-zero when the spared hero is the designated partner).

**Recommendation recorded, not acted on:** v39 is NOT an advance —
do not ship it on this evidence. The one-line fix is correct code
hygiene and should ride along inside a future candidate whose
headline mechanism earns its place (the turn-level lethal plan is
the named successor), rather than shipping alone on a metric it
does not move. The ship decision, as always, is Will's.

